OAI_PAPER({"slug": "Weil-classes-and-Hodge-classes-on-abelian-powers-October-6-2026", "title": "Weil classes and Hodge classes on abelian powers", "pdf": "https://github.com/openai/math/blob/main/preprints/Weil-classes-and-Hodge-classes-on-abelian-powers-October-6-2026/paper.pdf", "family": "032", "family_title": "The rational Hodge conjecture for CM abelian varieties", "abstract_html": "We prove the rational Hodge conjecture in every codimension on every self-power of a complex abelian sixfold with an imaginary-quadratic action and a compatible polarization whose rational homological Hermitian form is hyperbolic of signature \\((3,3)\\). We also prove it in every codimension on every self-power of a complex abelian variety of dimension at most five admitting an imaginary-quadratic action. Both results include nonsimple varieties and special periods with additional endomorphisms. The proof uses the companion theorem on the rational Hodge conjecture for CM abelian varieties.", "blocks": [{"label": "Theorem 1", "orig": "Theorem 1.1 (Split Weil sixfolds)", "depth": 0, "snippet": "The rational Hodge conjecture holds in every codimension on \\(A^m\\), for every \\(m\\geq1\\).", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(E=\\mathbb Q(u)\\) be an imaginary quadratic field, where \\(u^2=-d\\) and \\(d\\in\\mathbb Z_{&gt;0}\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(A\\) be a complex abelian sixfold with a unital embedding \\(E\\hookrightarrow \\operatorname{End}^0(A)\\).</p><p class=\"li\">A rational Riemann form \\(e\\) on \\(H_1(A,\\mathbb Q)\\) is compatible with \\(E\\) if\n  \\[\n  e(ax,y)=e(x,\\bar a y)\\qquad(a\\in E).\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Its associated homological Hermitian form, conjugate-linear in the first variable and linear in the second, is\n  \\[\n  h_E(x,y)=e(x,uy)+u e(x,y).\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Assume that a compatible polarization has actual rational Hermitian form \\(h_E\\) hyperbolic of signature \\((3,3)\\). Here hyperbolic means that \\(h_E\\) contains a totally isotropic three-dimensional \\(E\\)-subspace.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The rational Hodge conjecture holds in every codimension on \\(A^m\\), for every \\(m\\geq1\\). The assertion includes nonsimple varieties and periods with additional endomorphisms.</p>", "proof": "<p>A compatible rational polarization always exists for a quadratic action: starting with \\(e_0\\), set\n\\[\ne(x,y)=e_0(x,y)+d^{-1}e_0(ux,uy).\n\\]\nBoth summands are positive Riemann forms and \\(e(ux,y)=-e(x,uy)\\). For the present theorem retain the given compatible hyperbolic form.</p><p>Pass by isogeny to \\(\\prod_BB^{r_B}\\), transporting the action and pulling back the actual form. This preserves its Hermitian isometry class. Isogeny pullback and pushforward transfer the Hodge conjecture. The central isotypic projectors are selfadjoint, because a polarization identifies each simple type with its own dual type and there are no maps between distinct types. Thus isotypic summands are orthogonal for both \\(e\\) and \\(h_E\\), and their signatures add to \\((3,3)\\). A proper summand is not asserted hyperbolic.</p><p>Choose \\(H_0\\) as the CM isotypic sum and its embedding lines before testing determinant lists. Proposition 16 gives assumptions (a)–(c) of Proposition 2. Verify (d).</p><p>For each linear representative \\(W_j\\), let \\(n_j\\in\\mathbb Z\\) be its determinant multiplicity minus the opposite multiplicity. Opposite determinant pairs are algebraically supplied by Proposition 1. Orthogonal determinants have zero imbalance. For a fixed CM list, balance reads\n\\[\n\\sum_jn_j\\epsilon_\\gamma(W_j)+c_\\gamma=0\n\\qquad(\\gamma\\in\\operatorname{Gal}(k/\\mathbb Q)). \\tag{3.4}\n\\]\nOnly net exponents remain.</p><p>Types I and II need only pairs. For Type III dimension four, the column is an \\(E\\)-eigenspace of signature \\((2,2)\\), supplied by Proposition 15 for its restricted form. Dimension six exhausts \\(A\\), so the actual hyperbolic determinant is supplied. Admissible matrix-unit identifications transport this supply.</p><p>If Type IV has \\(E\\) not embedding in \\(K\\), it is a quadratic-center threefold repeated twice and exhausts \\(A\\). Its rank-three mixed column has nonzero imbalance, with no CM remainder; balance forces zero net exponent.</p><p>For split quadratic center \\(K=E\\), multiplicity-one factors in dimensions three, four, five have signature entries at most three by orthogonal addition to \\((3,3)\\). Proposition 15 supplies their determinants. The only repeated case is a threefold repeated twice; its central \\(E\\)-signature is \\((1,2)\\) or \\((2,1)\\), and the possibly noncentral given action changes only the assignment of column copies. Again supply holds. A sixfold factor exhausts \\(A\\), and the actual hyperbolic determinant supplies both orientations.</p><p>For the quartic-center fourfold \\((f,a,g)=(2,2,4)\\), choose orientation so its restricted signature is \\((1,3)\\). The two-dimensional complement has signature \\((2,0)\\). Its complex structure is a single \\(E\\)-scalar, so every \\(E\\)-line in an \\(E\\)-basis is a rational rank-one CM Hodge structure. It is isogenous to the square of an \\(E\\)-CM elliptic curve. Choose those embedding lines in \\(H_0\\); their imbalance sum \\(c_\\gamma\\) is fixed by automorphisms fixing \\(E\\).</p><p>The two column imbalances are \\((0,-2)\\), up to order. An automorphism exchanging the embeddings of \\(K/E\\), subtracted from the identity balance equation, gives \\(n_1=n_2\\). The remaining product is a power of \\(D(W_1)\\otimes D(W_2)\\), or its opposite, which algebraic direct-sum determinant maps identify with the full \\(E\\)-determinant of the fourfold, of signature \\((1,3)\\). Proposition 15 supplies it.</p><p>For higher-center sixfolds, the simple factor is all of \\(A\\), so \\(c_\\gamma=0\\).</p><p class=\"li\">For \\((f,a)=(2,3)\\), holomorphic multiplicities sum to three. The pure vectors \\((0,3),(3,0)\\) would make the factor CM. The remaining vector is \\((1,2)\\), with imbalance \\((-1,1)\\), so \\(n_1=n_2\\).</p><p class=\"li\">For \\((f,a)=(3,2)\\), multiplicities in \\(\\{0,1,2\\}\\) sum to three. The vector \\((1,1,1)\\) is excluded by the rank-two argument of Lemma 16.4. Thus the imbalance is a permutation of \\((-2,0,2)\\). Transitivity on three embeddings contains a three-cycle. Its three balance equations give\n  \\[\n  -n_1+n_3=0,\\qquad -n_2+n_1=0,\\qquad -n_3+n_2=0.\n  \\]\n  Hence all net exponents are equal.</p><p>In both higher-center cases, only a power of \\(\\bigotimes_{j=1}^fD(W_j)\\), or its opposite, remains. Admissible center projectors and alternation identify this with the full \\(E\\)-determinant of \\(A\\), supplied by its given hyperbolic form through Proposition 15.</p><p>These cases exhaust Proposition 16 and concern arbitrary integer exponents in every tensor degree. Products, opposite pairs, and determinant maps preserve CM supply by Proposition 1. Assumption (d) holds, and Proposition 2 proves the theorem on every self-power.</p>"}, {"label": "Theorem 2", "orig": "Theorem 1.2 (Quadratic actions in dimension at most five)", "depth": 0, "snippet": "The rational Hodge conjecture holds in every codimension on \\(A^m\\), for every \\(m\\geq1\\).", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(A\\) be a complex abelian variety of dimension at most five.</p><p class=\"li\">Assume that an imaginary quadratic field admits a unital embedding into \\(\\operatorname{End}^0(A)\\).</p><p class=\"li\">The action need not be the full endomorphism algebra and need not be central on a repeated simple factor.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The rational Hodge conjecture holds in every codimension on \\(A^m\\), for every \\(m\\geq1\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Average any rational Riemann form as in the first paragraph of Theorem Proof 1 to obtain a compatible polarization. Pass by isogeny to the simple-isotypic product and use Proposition 16 to obtain assumptions (a)–(c) of Proposition 2, with \\(H_0\\) the CM sum. Fix its embedding-line decomposition before testing lists.</p><p>Use the same net-exponent balance equations (3.4) and algebraic cancellation of opposite determinant pairs.</p><p>Types I and II require only pair tensors. A Type III factor has dimension four; its orthogonal column is admissibly isomorphic to an \\(E\\)-eigenspace of signature \\((2,2)\\). Proposition 15 supplies its determinant for every compatible rational form.</p><p>The Type IV case where \\(E\\) does not embed in the center is absent, since it requires a threefold with multiplicity two.</p><p>For quadratic center \\(K=E\\), mixed signatures in dimensions three and four have both entries at most three, so Proposition 15 supplies singleton determinants. A fivefold factor exhausts \\(A\\). Its single determinant imbalance is \\(2p-5\\neq0\\), and there is no CM remainder. Balance forces its net exponent to vanish; no determinant of signature \\((1,4)\\) or \\((4,1)\\) is required.</p><p>The only remaining case is \\((f,a,g)=(2,2,4)\\). Above a chosen \\(E\\)-embedding, one column is mixed and one pure, with imbalance vector \\((0,2)\\) or \\((0,-2)\\), up to order. A possible one-dimensional remainder inherits \\(E\\), so it is an \\(E\\)-CM elliptic curve. Choose its two \\(E\\)-embedding lines; automorphisms fixing \\(E\\) fix their imbalance contribution.</p><p>Subtract the balance equation for an automorphism exchanging the two embeddings of \\(K/E\\) from the identity equation:\n\\[\n(n_1-n_2)(d_1-d_2)=0.\n\\]\nSince \\(d_1\\neq d_2\\), \\(n_1=n_2\\). After opposite cancellation, the remaining product is a power of \\(D(W_1)\\otimes D(W_2)\\), or its opposite. Algebraic direct-sum determinant maps identify it with the full \\(E\\)-determinant of the fourfold, whose signature is \\((1,3)\\) or \\((3,1)\\). Proposition 15 supplies it.</p><p>Every balanced determinant list is therefore CM-supplied. Propositions 1 and 2 prove algebraicity of all rational Hodge tensors and hence the rational Hodge conjecture on every \\(A^m\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 1", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "CM supply is compatible with the algebraic operations needed for tensor invariants; rational Hodge classes in CM-supplied spaces are algebraic.", "statement": "<p>Algebraic supply, degree-one correspondences, and Hodge groups.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">All varieties are complex; all algebraic correspondences have rational coefficients unless scalar extension is explicitly indicated.</p><p class=\"li\">Put \\(k=\\overline{\\mathbb Q}\\subset\\mathbb C\\). Scalar automorphisms act on coefficients of the cohomology of a fixed variety, not on the variety.</p><p class=\"li\">Use \\(V(r)^{p,q}=V^{p+r,q+r}\\).</p><p class=\"li\">A CM abelian variety means an abelian variety whose degree-one Hodge group is a torus.</p><p class=\"li\">Use the companion input [30, Theorem 1.1]: the rational Hodge conjecture holds in every codimension on every complex CM abelian variety.</p><p class=\"li\">For a smooth projective \\(X\\), define\n  \\[\n  C^a(X)=\n  \\sum_{B,j,\\Gamma}\n  \\operatorname{im}\\!\\left(\n  \\Gamma_*:H^{a-2j}(B,\\mathbb Q)\\longrightarrow H^a(X,\\mathbb Q)\n  \\right),\n  \\quad\n  \\Gamma\\in CH^{\\dim B+j}(B\\times X)_{\\mathbb Q},\n  \\]\n  where \\(B\\) ranges over CM abelian varieties, including a point.</p><p class=\"li\">A subspace of \\(H^a(X,k)\\) is CM-supplied if it is contained in \\(C^a(X)\\otimes_{\\mathbb Q}k\\).</p><p class=\"li\">If \\(X\\) is an abelian variety or a smooth connected projective curve, a subspace \\(W\\subset H^1(X,k)\\) is admissible if it is the image of a \\(k\\)-linear idempotent whose every scalar conjugate preserves Hodge types. A map between such spaces is admissible if it preserves Hodge types after every scalar conjugation.</p><p class=\"li\">Write \\(\\overline W\\) for scalar complex conjugation.</p><p class=\"li\">A pair tensor between \\(W,W'\\) is a tensor \\(t\\in W\\otimes W'\\) defining an isomorphism \\(W^*\\to W'\\), with every scalar conjugate of type \\((1,1)\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Set\n  \\[\n  D(W)=\\bigwedge^{\\dim W}W\\subset W^{\\otimes\\dim W},\n  \\qquad D(0)=k.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>CM supply is compatible with the algebraic operations needed for tensor invariants; rational Hodge classes in CM-supplied spaces are algebraic. Admissible degree-one maps and pairings are algebraic. Toric rational degree-one summands are realized by CM isogeny factors. Determinant factorization, pairing cancellation, and symplectic determinant supply are algebraic operations.</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemmas 1.1–1.4 establish the supply, realization, and Hodge-group assertions.</p><p>For the determinant operations, admissible inclusions and projections, followed by alternation, give nonzero algebraic maps in both directions between\n\\[\nD\\!\\left(\\bigoplus_jW_j\\right)\n\\quad\\text{and}\\quad\n\\bigotimes_jD(W_j).\n\\]\nOrdinary slot permutations differ from geometric permutations only by rational Koszul signs.</p><p>If \\(W,W'\\) have a pair tensor, the determinant of its inverse pairing contracts \\(D(W)\\otimes D(W')\\) nontrivially to the unit line. Conversely, alternating the appropriate tensor power of the pair tensor supplies this product algebraically. An alternating self-pair on a \\(2r\\)-space supplies its determinant by its \\(r\\)-th exterior power. Nonvanishing is the determinant, respectively Pfaffian, calculation in bases.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.1", "orig": "Lemma 2.1 (Operations and Hodge lifts)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "In the setting of Proposition 1, CM supply is preserved by external products, permutations, cup products, rational algebraic correspondences, \\(k\\)-linear combinations of such correspondences, and scalar conjugation.", "statement": "<p>In the setting of Proposition 1, CM supply is preserved by external products, permutations, cup products, rational algebraic correspondences, \\(k\\)-linear combinations of such correspondences, and scalar conjugation. Every rational Hodge class in a CM-supplied subspace is algebraic.</p>", "proof": "<p>External products of correspondence cycles realize products of their maps, and a product of CM abelian varieties is CM. Composition is realized by pullback to the triple product, intersection, and pushforward. Graphs give pullback and pushforward; diagonal pullback gives cup product, and integration gives contraction. Linearity gives the scalar assertion. Scalar conjugation preserves \\(C^a(X)\\otimes k\\), since \\(C^a(X)\\) is rational.</p><p>Let \\(\\alpha\\in H^{2p}(X,\\mathbb Q)\\) be of type \\((p,p)\\) and belong to \\(C^{2p}(X)\\otimes k\\). Membership in a rational subspace descends under field extension. Finitely many correspondence maps therefore give a morphism of polarizable pure weight-\\(2p\\) Hodge structures\n\\[\nf:\\bigoplus_{\\nu=1}^s\nH^{2p-2j_\\nu}(B_\\nu,\\mathbb Q)(-j_\\nu)\n\\longrightarrow H^{2p}(X,\\mathbb Q)\n\\]\nwhose image contains \\(\\alpha\\).</p><p>The orthogonal complement of \\(\\ker f\\) for a polarization is a rational Hodge complement and maps isomorphically onto \\(\\operatorname{im}f\\). Nondegeneracy on \\(\\ker f\\) follows from the positive Hermitian form obtained using the Weil operator. Hence \\(\\alpha\\) has a rational Hodge lift. Before twisting, its \\(\\nu\\)-th component has type \\((p-j_\\nu,p-j_\\nu)\\) on \\(B_\\nu\\), and is algebraic by the companion CM theorem. Applying the correspondence cycles and adding proves algebraicity of \\(\\alpha\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.2", "orig": "Lemma 2.2 (Algebraic maps and contractions)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "In the degree-one setting of Proposition 1:", "statement": "<p>In the degree-one setting of Proposition 1:</p><p class=\"li num\">1. Every admissible map is induced on the indicated summands by a \\(k\\)-linear combination of rational algebraic correspondences.</p><p class=\"li num\">2. Every pair tensor is algebraic over \\(k\\), and contraction by its inverse pairing is algebraic.</p><p class=\"li num\">3. If \\(W\\) is admissible, then \\(\\overline W\\) is admissible and there is a pair tensor between \\(W\\) and \\(\\overline W\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>First record coefficient descent. If \\(T\\) is rational, \\(P\\subset T_{\\mathbb C}\\) is complex-linear, and \\(t\\in T_k\\) satisfies \\(\\gamma t\\in P\\) for every \\(\\gamma\\in\\operatorname{Gal}(k/\\mathbb Q)\\), then\n\\[\nt\\in(T\\cap P)\\otimes_{\\mathbb Q}k. \\tag{2.1}\n\\]\nChoose a finite Galois field \\(K\\) containing the coefficients of \\(t\\). For \\(a\\in K\\),\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Tr}_{K/\\mathbb Q}(at)\n=\\sum_{\\tau\\in\\operatorname{Gal}(K/\\mathbb Q)}\n\\tau(a)\\tau(t)\n\\]\nis rational and belongs to \\(P\\). Taking \\(a\\) from the trace-dual basis extracts the rational coefficients of \\(t\\), proving (2.1).</p><p>Extend an admissible map by its source projector and target inclusion. Equation (2.1) expresses it as a \\(k\\)-linear combination of rational Hodge maps. A rational degree-one Hodge map between abelian varieties is induced contravariantly by a rational homomorphism: dualize to homology, clear lattice denominators, and use the resulting complex-linear map of complex tori. Such a homomorphism is algebraic. For a curve target use its Abel map; for a curve source, the cup pairing identifies the map with a rational \\((1,1)\\)-class on the product, hence a divisor class by Lefschetz \\((1,1)\\).</p><p>A pair tensor lies in \\(H^1\\otimes H^1\\subset H^2\\) of the product. Coefficient descent and Lefschetz \\((1,1)\\) make it algebraic over \\(k\\). Let \\(b:W\\otimes W'\\to k\\) be the inverse pairing, with bidegree \\((-1,-1)\\), extended by the admissible projectors. For a rational polarization pairing \\(\\psi_X\\), write\n\\[\nb(x,y)=\\psi_X(x,v(y)).\n\\]\nIn every scalar conjugate, complementary Hodge degrees are paired; nondegeneracy of \\(\\psi_X\\) makes \\(v\\) admissible. Algebraize \\(v\\); contraction by \\(\\psi_X\\) is cup product, an ample power if necessary, and integration.</p><p>The conjugate projector makes \\(\\overline W\\) admissible. The polarization restricts perfectly to \\(W\\times\\overline W\\): its positive Hermitian form detects every nonzero vector in the Hodge-stable space \\(W_{\\mathbb C}\\). Its inverse gives a tensor in \\(W\\otimes\\overline W\\). Conjugating the nonzero determinant proves nondegeneracy in every scalar conjugate; the conjugate pairing still pairs complementary Hodge degrees. This does not assume that scalar conjugation commutes with complex conjugation.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.3", "orig": "Lemma 2.3 (Hodge tensors determine the Hodge group)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Let \\(H\\) be a polarizable rational weight-one Hodge structure with types \\((1,0),(0,1)\\).", "statement": "<p>Let \\(H\\) be a polarizable rational weight-one Hodge structure with types \\((1,0),(0,1)\\). Let \\(J=\\operatorname{Hdg}(H)\\), the smallest rational algebraic subgroup containing the Hodge circle, acting by \\(z,\\bar z\\) on these two types.</p><p>Inside the symplectic group of a polarization, \\(J\\) is the pointwise stabilizer of all rational tensors of type \\((a,a)\\) in \\(H^{\\otimes2a}\\), for all \\(a\\geq0\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>The identity component of \\(J\\) is rational and contains the connected Hodge circle; minimality makes \\(J\\) connected. Polarizability gives rational Hodge complements to rational invariant subspaces, and minimality makes these complements invariant. Thus \\(J\\) is reductive.</p><p>By minimality, rational \\(J\\)-invariant tensors are exactly rational circle-invariant tensors. Chevalley’s theorem gives a rational \\(GL(H)\\)-representation \\(T\\) and a line \\(L\\subset T\\) whose stabilizer is \\(J\\). Reductivity supplies a \\(J\\)-equivariant projector \\(e:T\\to L\\).</p><p>Rational \\(GL(H)\\)-representations are subquotients of sums of mixed tensor powers; complete reducibility embeds \\(\\operatorname{End}(T)\\) as a direct summand of such a sum. Use the polarization to replace dual factors by ordinary factors, equivariantly for the ambient symplectic group. The components of \\(e\\) become rational circle-invariant tensors, hence balanced tensors in even degree. Any symplectic element fixing all such tensors fixes \\(e\\), preserves \\(L\\), and belongs to \\(J\\). The reverse inclusion follows from minimality.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.4", "orig": "Lemma 2.4 (Toric degree-one summands)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Let \\(A\\) be a complex abelian variety and \\(H\\subset H^1(A,\\mathbb Q)\\) a rational Hodge summand.", "statement": "<p>Let \\(A\\) be a complex abelian variety and \\(H\\subset H^1(A,\\mathbb Q)\\) a rational Hodge summand. If \\(\\operatorname{Hdg}(H)\\) is a torus, then\n\\[\nH\\simeq H^1(B,\\mathbb Q)\n\\]\nfor a CM isogeny factor \\(B\\) of \\(A\\). Its scalar extension admits a decomposition into one-dimensional CM embedding lines, each pure of type \\((1,0)\\) or \\((0,1)\\), permuted by scalar conjugation.</p>", "proof": "<p>A rational Hodge projector onto \\(H\\) is induced by a rational endomorphism of \\(A\\), by Lemma 1.2. Clear denominators and take its image. This gives an abelian isogeny factor \\(B\\) with the required first cohomology; its Hodge group is toric.</p><p>Let \\(E_0\\) be the envelope of this torus in \\(\\operatorname{End}_{\\mathbb Q}(H)\\). After splitting the torus, this is an algebra of diagonal scalars, hence commutative semisimple. The polarization adjoint preserves \\(E_0\\) and is positive. A positive involution on a real finite étale algebra fixes primitive real summands and is complex conjugation on complex summands: exchanging idempotents would give \\(e^*e=0\\), and the identity involution on a complex summand would give \\(i^*i=-1\\).</p><p>A real summand is impossible, because the connected compact circle would act trivially there, whereas the only weight-one characters are \\(z,\\bar z\\). Thus the rational field factors are CM fields. Split their modules into field lines using rational Hodge projectors; Lemma 1.2 realizes these by abelian factors. For a rank-one module over a CM field \\(F\\), the idempotents of\n\\[\nF\\otimes_{\\mathbb Q}k=\\prod_{\\sigma:F\\hookrightarrow k}k\n\\]\ngive the stated embedding lines.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 2", "orig": "Theorem 2.5 (A determinant criterion for all powers)", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Every rational balanced tensor in \\(H^{\\otimes2p}\\) is algebraic on separate \\(A\\)-slots.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(A\\) be a complex abelian variety, \\(H=H^1(A,\\mathbb Q)\\), \\(J=\\operatorname{Hdg}(H)\\), and \\(R=J^{\\mathrm{der}}\\).</p><p class=\"li\">For admissible \\(W\\), define\n  \\[\n  \\epsilon_\\gamma(W)=\n  \\dim_{\\mathbb C}(\\gamma W_{\\mathbb C}\\cap H^{1,0})\n  -\n  \\dim_{\\mathbb C}(\\gamma W_{\\mathbb C}\\cap H^{0,1}).\n  \\]\n  Proposition 1 gives\n  \\[\n  \\epsilon_\\gamma(\\overline W)=-\\epsilon_\\gamma(W). \\tag{2.2}\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Assume:</p><p class=\"li l2 num\">1. A rational Hodge summand \\(H_0\\subset H\\) has toric Hodge group. Choose a decomposition of \\(H_{0,k}\\) into CM embedding lines, distinguishing repetitions; call its list \\(\\mathcal E_0\\).</p><p class=\"li l2 num\">2. The complement is a direct sum of admissible classical blocks. In a symplectic or orthogonal block every space is admissibly identified with an actual representative \\(V_i\\), carrying an alternating or symmetric self-pair tensor. In a linear block, \\(\\dim V_i\\geq2\\), and every space is admissibly identified with \\(V_i\\) or \\(\\overline V_i\\), paired with each other. Thus \\(\\overline V_i\\simeq V_i^*(-1)\\) as Hodge-graded spaces and is the derived dual representation.</p><p class=\"li l2 num\">3. The image of \\(R_k\\) contains\n     \\[\n     P=\n     \\prod_{i\\in I_{\\mathrm{Sp}}}Sp(V_i)\n     \\times\n     \\prod_{i\\in I_{\\mathrm{SO}}}SO(V_i)\n     \\times\n     \\prod_{i\\in I_{\\mathrm{SL}}}SL(V_i),\n     \\]\n     acting independently on its own copies, dually on opposite linear copies, and trivially on \\(H_{0,k}\\) and other blocks.</p><p class=\"li l2 num\">4. For every list \\(W_1,\\ldots,W_s\\), with repetitions, from\n     \\[\n     \\{V_i:i\\in I_{\\mathrm{SO}}\\}\n     \\cup\n     \\{V_i,\\overline V_i:i\\in I_{\\mathrm{SL}}\\},\n     \\]\n     whenever one fixed list \\(\\ell_1,\\ldots,\\ell_t\\in\\mathcal E_0\\), also with repetitions, satisfies\n     \\[\n     \\sum_{j=1}^s\\epsilon_\\gamma(W_j)\n     +\n     \\sum_{a=1}^t\\epsilon_\\gamma(\\ell_a)=0\n     \\quad\\text{for every }\\gamma\\in\\operatorname{Gal}(k/\\mathbb Q), \\tag{2.3}\n     \\]\n     the line \\(\\bigotimes_jD(W_j)\\) is CM-supplied on separate tensor slots.</p><p class=\"li\">Empty lists are permitted.</p><p class=\"li\">Call the data saturated if every singleton determinant for an orthogonal or linear representative is CM-supplied without a balance condition.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Every rational balanced tensor in \\(H^{\\otimes2p}\\) is algebraic on separate \\(A\\)-slots. Consequently the rational Hodge conjecture holds on every \\(A^m\\). For saturated data and any CM abelian variety \\(M\\), the induced data on \\(A\\times M\\) are saturated, and the same conclusions hold on every power of \\(A\\times M\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Use the all-degree multilinear first fundamental theorems:</p><p class=\"li\">\\(Sp(V)\\)-invariants are spanned by permutations of products of the inverse alternating-form tensor, and vanish in odd degree.</p><p class=\"li\">\\(SO(V)\\)-invariants are spanned by products of the inverse symmetric-form tensor and a top alternating tensor.</p><p class=\"li\">\\(SL(V)\\)-invariants in mixed powers of \\(V,V^*\\) are spanned by identity coevaluations and the two top alternating tensors.</p><p>These are spanning statements in every degree, and hold over \\(k\\) by scalar extension. Decompose each tensor slot into block summands and apply them independently to the factors of \\(P\\).</p><p>A rational balanced tensor is fixed by \\(J\\), hence by \\(P\\). In its fixed tensor degree, it is therefore in the span of finitely many lines formed from CM embedding lines, pair tensors, orthogonal determinants, and either orientation of linear determinants, followed by admissible identifications and slot permutations.</p><p>Each such line is pure in every scalar conjugate. Lemma 2.1 retains only lines balanced in every conjugate. Removing the \\((1,1)\\)-pair factors from the bidegree count leaves precisely (2.3), so the determinant product is CM-supplied. Proposition 1 supplies the toric lines from a CM isogeny factor and algebraizes the pair tensors and identifications. Thus every retained generator belongs to the scalar extension of the rational CM-supplied space. Rational membership descends, and Proposition 1 makes the original tensor algebraic.</p><p>For \\(A^m\\), replace \\(H\\) by \\(H^{\\oplus m}\\); this only repeats blocks, and repetitions were already allowed. The identity\n\\[\nH^{2p}(A^m,\\mathbb Q)=\\bigwedge^{2p}H^1(A^m,\\mathbb Q)\n\\]\nand normalized antisymmetrization give a rational Hodge lift to ordinary tensor powers. Algebraize that lift on separate slots and pull back along the diagonal to recover the original class.</p><p>For saturated data, conjugation supplies opposite determinants and products supply every determinant list. If \\(T_M=\\operatorname{Hdg}(H^1(M,\\mathbb Q))\\), then\n\\[\nJ'=\\operatorname{Hdg}(H\\oplus H^1(M,\\mathbb Q))\n\\subset J\\times T_M\n\\]\nis connected reductive and projects onto \\(J\\). Its derived group projects onto \\(R\\) and trivially onto \\(T_M\\); inside this representation,\n\\[\n(J')^{\\mathrm{der}}=R\\times\\{1\\}.\n\\]\nThe old classical product still acts independently, the toric summand enlarges, and the supplied singleton determinants pull back algebraically. This proves saturation and the assertions for \\(A\\times M\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Simultaneous balance observation from the proof of Theorem 2.5.", "statement": "<p>Simultaneous balance observation from the proof of Theorem 2.5.</p><p>Let \\(T\\) be a rational pure weight-\\(2p\\) Hodge structure. Let \\(F\\subset T_k\\) be spanned by finitely many lines \\(L_a\\), each pure in Hodge bidegree after every scalar conjugation. A rational \\((p,p)\\)-vector \\(\\alpha\\in F\\) is spanned by those \\(L_a\\) whose every scalar conjugate has type \\((p,p)\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>For fixed \\(\\gamma\\), group the original lines according to the Hodge bidegree of \\(\\gamma L_a\\). Their group sums form a direct sum, as follows after applying \\(\\gamma\\) and using the Hodge decomposition. Let \\(q_\\gamma\\) retain the group of type \\((p,p)\\). Since \\(\\gamma\\alpha=\\alpha\\), this projection fixes \\(\\alpha\\).</p><p>Every \\(L_a\\) is an eigenline of every \\(q_\\gamma\\), with eigenvalue zero or one. These projections commute even if the spanning lines are dependent. Only finitely many distinct projections occur, because each is determined by a subset of the finite list. Their product fixes \\(\\alpha\\) and has image spanned by exactly the lines retained by every scalar conjugation.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 3", "orig": "Lemma 2.6 (Correspondence transfer)", "depth": 0, "snippet": "The rational Hodge conjecture holds on every finite product of the \\(Y_i\\), with repetitions and additional factors from \\(\\mathcal S\\).", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\mathcal S\\) be a collection of smooth projective varieties such that the rational Hodge conjecture holds on every finite product of members of \\(\\mathcal S\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(Y_1,\\ldots,Y_v\\) be smooth projective.</p><p class=\"li\">For every \\(i,q\\), assume \\(H^q(Y_i,\\mathbb Q)\\) is spanned by images\n  \\[\n  \\Gamma_*:H^{q-2j}(S,\\mathbb Q)\\longrightarrow H^q(Y_i,\\mathbb Q),\n  \\quad\n  \\Gamma\\in CH^{\\dim S+j}(S\\times Y_i)_{\\mathbb Q},\n  \\quad S\\in\\mathcal S.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The rational Hodge conjecture holds on every finite product of the \\(Y_i\\), with repetitions and additional factors from \\(\\mathcal S\\).</p><p>The conclusion also holds if the spanning is given over \\(k\\) by restrictions of maps induced by \\(k\\)-linear combinations of rational correspondences, provided their images span the full target degree after scalar extension.</p>", "proof": "<p>Finite dimensionality makes a finite sum of the prescribed maps surjective in each degree. Tensor these maps and the identities of additional source factors. Künneth shows that finite sums of external-product correspondence maps from finite products of members of \\(\\mathcal S\\) surject onto every target degree. No algebraic Künneth projector on a source is required.</p><p>For degree \\(2p\\), these maps give a surjection of polarizable pure weight-\\(2p\\) Hodge structures\n\\[\n\\bigoplus_\\nu H^{2p-2j_\\nu}(S_\\nu,\\mathbb Q)(-j_\\nu)\n\\longrightarrow H^{2p}(Y,\\mathbb Q).\n\\]\nThe polarization splitting used in Proposition 1 lifts a rational Hodge class. Its components before twisting are rational \\((p-j_\\nu,p-j_\\nu)\\)-classes and are algebraic by the source hypothesis. Their correspondence images are algebraic.</p><p>For scalar correspondences, expand each into finitely many rational cycles. The image of the restriction is contained in the scalar extension of the sum of the full rational images. If scalar images span the target, the rational quotient becomes zero after scalar extension and was already zero.</p><p>In particular, if \\(A\\) has saturated data, the varieties \\(A^a\\times M\\), with \\(M\\) CM, satisfy the source-product hypothesis by Proposition 2 and algebraic factor inclusions and projections. If \\(A=\\operatorname{Jac}(C)\\), the Abel map supplies \\(H^1(C)\\), and a point and the fundamental class supply its even cohomology. Varieties \\(F\\) satisfying \\(C^q(F)=H^q(F,\\mathbb Q)\\) in all degrees are supplied by finitely many CM correspondence images. External products and the proved transfer give the corresponding conclusions for products of such \\(C\\) and \\(F\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 4", "orig": "Lemma 3.2 (A rational hyperbolicity test)", "depth": 0, "snippet": "The form \\(h\\) is hyperbolic if and only if \\( [\\det h]=[(-1)^b]. \\) Every hyperbolic form of this rank is isometric to a sum of \\(b\\) planes with Gram matrix \\( \\begin{pmatrix}0&1\\\\1&0\\end{pmatrix}. \\)", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(E\\) be imaginary quadratic.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(h\\) be a nondegenerate \\(E\\)-Hermitian form of rank \\(2b\\), signature \\((b,b)\\), with \\(b\\geq1\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Write\n  \\[\n  [\\det h]\\in\\mathbb Q^\\times/\\operatorname{Nm}_{E/\\mathbb Q}(E^\\times).\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The form \\(h\\) is hyperbolic if and only if\n\\[\n[\\det h]=[(-1)^b].\n\\]\nEvery hyperbolic form of this rank is isometric to a sum of \\(b\\) planes with Gram matrix\n\\[\n\\begin{pmatrix}0&amp;1\\\\1&amp;0\\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>For necessity, choose \\(0\\neq v\\) in a totally isotropic \\(b\\)-space \\(L\\), choose \\(w\\) with \\(h(v,w)=1\\), and replace \\(w\\) by\n\\[\nw-\\tfrac12h(w,w)v.\n\\]\nThe pair spans a hyperbolic plane. For \\(x\\in L\\), replace \\(x\\) by \\(x-h(w,x)v\\); this projects \\(L\\) to an isotropic \\((b-1)\\)-space in the orthogonal complement. Induction gives the claimed decomposition, each plane having determinant \\(-1\\).</p><p>Conversely, \\(x\\mapsto h(x,x)\\) is a nondegenerate rational quadratic form of dimension \\(4b\\) and signature \\((2b,2b)\\). For \\(b\\geq2\\), Meyer’s theorem supplies a nonzero isotropic vector. Split off a Hermitian hyperbolic plane. The complement has signature \\((b-1,b-1)\\) and determinant class \\([(-1)^{b-1}]\\); repeat to rank two.</p><p>Write the last form as \\(\\langle a,b'\\rangle\\), with \\(a,b'\\in\\mathbb Q^\\times\\). Its determinant condition gives \\(-ab'=\\operatorname{Nm}(t)\\). Then \\((t/a,1)\\) is isotropic, since\n\\[\na\\operatorname{Nm}(t/a)+b'=0.\n\\]\nIt splits the last hyperbolic plane.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 5", "orig": "Proposition 4.1 (Simultaneous equivariant seeds)", "depth": 0, "snippet": "There exist such \\(C,p\\), cyclic subgroups \\(G_1,G_2\\subset X\\) of order \\(n\\) with \\(G_1\\cap G_2=0\\), and cosets \\( \\{s_1,\\ldots,s_n\\}=s_0+G_2,\\qquad \\{t_1,\\ldots,t_n\\}=t_0+G_1, \\) such that …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Fix an integer \\(d\\geq3\\), and put \\(n=d+1\\).</p><p class=\"li\">For a smooth nonhyperelliptic genus-three curve \\(C\\) and \\(p\\in C\\), give \\(X=\\operatorname{Pic}^2(C)\\) origin \\(\\mathcal O_C(2p)\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Put\n  \\[\n  \\Theta=W_2(C),\\quad H=\\mathcal O_X(\\Theta),\\quad \\widehat X=\\operatorname{Pic}^0(X),\n  \\]\n  \\[\n  C_p=\\{\\mathcal O_C(p+q):q\\in C\\},\\qquad\n  \\Sigma_p=\\{\\omega_C(-p-q):q\\in C\\}.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Translation is \\(t_v(z)=z+v\\); \\(t_vD\\) denotes image translation.</p><p class=\"li\">The normalized Betti class satisfies\n  \\[\n  [C_p]=[\\Sigma_p]=\\frac{\\Theta^2}{2},\\qquad\n  \\Theta\\cdot C_p=\\Theta\\cdot\\Sigma_p=3.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There exist such \\(C,p\\), cyclic subgroups \\(G_1,G_2\\subset X\\) of order \\(n\\) with \\(G_1\\cap G_2=0\\), and cosets\n\\[\n\\{s_1,\\ldots,s_n\\}=s_0+G_2,\\qquad\n\\{t_1,\\ldots,t_n\\}=t_0+G_1,\n\\]\nsuch that\n\\[\nA=\\coprod_i t_{s_i}C_p,\\qquad\n\\Sigma=\\coprod_j t_{t_j}\\Sigma_p\n\\]\nare disjoint within each displayed union, and\n\\[\nH^0(X,I_AH^{\\otimes2}N)\n=\nH^0(X,I_\\Sigma H^{\\otimes2}N)=0\n\\qquad(N\\in\\widehat X). \\tag{4.10}\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Begin with the compact-type stable curve\n\\[\nC_0=R\\cup E_1\\cup E_2\\cup E_3,\\qquad R\\simeq\\mathbb P^1,\n\\]\nwhere each elliptic tail meets \\(R\\) once, at distinct nodes \\(e_i\\). Choose a projective one-parameter smoothing \\(\\mathcal C\\to S\\), with regular total surface and smooth nonhyperelliptic noncentral fibers.</p><p>For existence, the node cotangent presentations have length one, the local \\(\\operatorname{Ext}^1\\) is supported at the nodes, and \\(\\operatorname{Ext}^2(\\Omega_{C_0},\\mathcal O_{C_0})=0\\). The normalized-component tangent contributions are zero for \\(T_R(-e_1-e_2-e_3)=\\mathcal O_{\\mathbb P^1}(-1)\\), and one-dimensional for each \\(T_{E_i}(-e_i)\\). Together with three smoothing parameters, the versal germ is smooth of dimension six. The stable-curve stack supplies an algebraic chart. The genus-three hyperelliptic locus has dimension five; a general algebraic curve through the chosen point avoids its closure generically and has nonzero derivatives in all node parameters. Locally \\(uv=t\\) times a unit, giving regularity. After étale localization choose a section \\(\\sigma\\) through a smooth point of \\(R\\).</p><p>The componentwise degree-zero relative Picard space \\(J\\) is separated, smooth, and of finite type, with semiabelian fibers. The normalization sequence gives\n\\[\n1\\to\\mathbb G_m^{b_1(\\Delta)}\n\\to\\operatorname{Pic}^0(C_0)\n\\to\\prod_v\\operatorname{Pic}^0(C_v)\\to1.\n\\]\nThe graph is a tree, so \\(J_0=\\prod_i\\operatorname{Pic}^0(E_i)\\). Proper connected fibers make \\(J\\) proper; the generic canonical polarization extends over the normal base and makes \\(J\\) projective. Its degree is one, so \\(J\\) is a principally polarized abelian scheme.</p><p>To identify the central polarization, use a universal line \\(\\mathcal U\\) rigidified along \\(\\sigma\\), and define\n\\[\n\\mathcal T=\n\\left(\\det Rp_{C,*}(\\mathcal U\\otimes\\mathcal O_{\\mathcal C}(2\\sigma))\\right)^{-1}.\n\\]\nLemma 5.3 defines this and its base changes. On a smooth fiber, its determinant section vanishes on \\(W_2\\), with generic multiplicity one: the first-order differential is cup product\n\\[\nH^1(\\mathcal O_C)\\to\\operatorname{Hom}(H^0(N),H^1(N)),\n\\]\nwhose dual is nonzero multiplication\n\\(H^0(N)\\otimes H^0(\\omega_CN^{-1})\\to H^0(\\omega_C)\\).\nThus \\(\\varphi_{\\mathcal T}\\) is the generic canonical polarization, and separatedness identifies it with the extended one.</p><p>On \\(C_0\\), normalization factors this determinant, up to constant lines, as\n\\[\n\\mathcal T_0\\simeq\n\\boxtimes_i\\left(\\det R\\operatorname{pr}_{\\widehat E_i,*}\\mathcal P_i\\right)^{-1}.\n\\]\nEach elliptic factor is \\(\\mathcal O_{\\widehat E_i}(0)\\), by its cohomology and the nonzero first-order cup product at the origin. Hence the central polarization is the product polarization.</p><p>Set \\(\\mathcal D=(1,\\lambda_J)^*\\mathcal P_J\\). The biextension identity gives \\(\\varphi_{\\mathcal D}=2\\lambda_J\\). Consequently \\(\\mathcal D_s\\) differs from \\(2\\Theta_s\\) by a degree-zero line, and centrally \\(\\mathcal D_0=D_1\\boxtimes D_2\\boxtimes D_3\\) with \\(\\deg D_i=2\\).</p><p>The pointed Abel morphism extends over \\(\\mathcal C\\). Resolve its indeterminacy on the regular surface; exceptional rational trees map constantly to the abelian central fiber. The graph image is finite birational over the normal surface and therefore an isomorphism. For \\(q_0\\in E_i\\), the tail-twisted line\n\\[\n\\mathcal O_{\\mathcal C}(q-\\sigma+E_i)\n\\]\nhas componentwise degree zero because\n\\[\n\\mathcal O(E_i)|_{E_i}=\\mathcal O_{E_i}(-e_i),\\quad\n\\mathcal O(E_i)|_R=\\mathcal O_R(e_i),\n\\]\nand has trivial restrictions to other tails. Thus the central Abel image has support the three-axis star \\(Z_0\\), with \\(R\\) contracted to zero.</p><p>Take the scheme-theoretic closure \\(\\mathcal A\\) of the generic Abel image. Its quotient is torsion-free over the base DVR, hence flat, and its central support is \\(Z_0\\). On smooth fibers the relative Abel morphism is a closed immersion: it is proper with finite fibers, hence finite, and fiberwise surjectivity of the structure-sheaf map implies total surjectivity by Nakayama. Therefore the noncentral closure fibers are the actual Abel curves.</p><p>Embed \\(H_n=(\\mathbb Z/n)^2\\) diagonally into \\(\\prod_iE_i[n]\\), using isomorphisms \\(H_n\\simeq E_i[n]\\). Its two cyclic direct factors extend, after étale localization, to torsion sections with the same orders and trivial intersection. Each central projection is injective.</p><p>For one cyclic factor, central translates of \\(\\mathcal A\\) are disjoint by Lemma 5.2. Properness permits removal of the finitely many intersection images, so their union \\(\\mathcal Z\\) is closed and flat near zero. Repeat with \\(-\\mathcal A\\) for the other factor.</p><p>The ideal \\(I_{\\mathcal Z}\\) is \\(S\\)-flat. On \\(J\\times_S\\widehat J\\), the sheaf\n\\[\n\\operatorname{pr}_J^*(I_{\\mathcal Z}\\mathcal D)\\otimes\\mathcal P_J\n\\]\nis \\(\\widehat J\\)-flat. Lemma 5.3 makes the locus of nonzero \\(H^0\\) closed. Its central fiber is empty by Lemma 5.2, even if the limiting star is thickened. Properness of \\(\\widehat J/S\\) makes its image closed, so shrinking gives vanishing for every Picard twist. Repeat for the negative orbit.</p><p>Choose a noncentral fiber. Its positive Abel curve is \\(C_p\\); its negative Abel curve differs from \\(\\Sigma_p\\) by a fixed translate. Degree-zero discrepancies in the twice-theta line are absorbed by arbitrary \\(N\\). This proves the proposition.</p><p>Finally, a translate of \\(C_p\\) cannot equal a translate of \\(\\Sigma_p\\): equality would give a fixed degree-two line with an effective divisor containing every varying \\(q\\), hence two independent sections, contradicting nonhyperellipticity. Thus curves from opposite parts have only finite or empty intersection.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.1", "orig": "Lemma 4.2 (Singular marked points of a trilinear form)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Let \\(V_1,V_2,V_3\\) be two-dimensional vector spaces and \\(0\\neq T\\in V_1\\otimes V_2\\otimes V_3\\).", "statement": "<p>Let \\(V_1,V_2,V_3\\) be two-dimensional vector spaces and \\(0\\neq T\\in V_1\\otimes V_2\\otimes V_3\\). Let\n\\[\n(x_h,y_h,z_h)\\in\\mathbb P(V_1^*)\\times\\mathbb P(V_2^*)\\times\\mathbb P(V_3^*)\n\\]\nbe a finite indexed collection with at most two indices in every fiber of each coordinate projection.</p><p>At most three indices satisfy\n\\[\nT(-,y_h,z_h)=T(x_h,-,z_h)=T(x_h,y_h,-)=0. \\tag{4.11}\n\\]\nIndices, not distinct triples, are counted.</p>", "proof": "<p>Write \\(T(x,y,z)=y^tM(x)z\\), where \\(M(x)=x_0A+x_1B\\), and \\(D(x)=\\det M(x)\\). The equations imply\n\\[\nM(x)z=0,\\qquad y^tM(x)=0,\\qquad y^tAz=y^tBz=0.\n\\]\nRank two is impossible. In rank one, the adjugate is a nonzero multiple of \\(zy^t\\), so the determinant derivative formula gives \\(\\partial_{x_0}D=\\partial_{x_1}D=0\\). The same holds when \\(M(x)=0\\).</p><p>If \\(D\\neq0\\), its gradient is a nonzero linear map and has at most one projective zero. All qualifying indices have the same \\(x\\), so there are at most two.</p><p>If \\(D=0\\), every nonzero pencil matrix has rank one. A one-dimensional pencil makes \\(T\\) a product of three linear forms. In a two-dimensional pencil write \\(A=uv^t\\), \\(B=u'v'^t\\); then\n\\[\n0=\\det(A+B)=\\det[u,u']\\det[v,v']\n\\]\ngives a common left or right factor. After permuting variables, \\(T=l(x)q(y,z)\\). If \\(q\\) has rank two, the equations force \\(l(x)=0\\), giving at most two indices. If \\(q\\) has rank one, write \\(T=l(x)m(y)k(z)\\). At least two of these three factors must vanish. Each distinguished coordinate value occurs at most twice; if \\(N\\) indices qualify, \\(2N\\leq6\\), hence \\(N\\leq3\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.2", "orig": "Lemma 4.3 (The elliptic star)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Let \\(J_0=E_1\\times E_2\\times E_3\\) be a product of elliptic curves, and \\(Z_0\\) the reduced union of the three coordinate elliptic axes.", "statement": "<p>Let \\(J_0=E_1\\times E_2\\times E_3\\) be a product of elliptic curves, and \\(Z_0\\) the reduced union of the three coordinate elliptic axes. Let \\(K\\subset J_0\\) be finite of order at least four, projecting injectively to every \\(E_i\\).</p><p>The translates \\(Z(g)=g+Z_0\\), \\(g\\in K\\), are pairwise disjoint. For degree-two lines \\(D_i\\) on \\(E_i\\),\n\\[\nH^0\\!\\left(J_0,I_{\\bigcup_{g\\in K}Z(g)}\n(D_1\\boxtimes D_2\\boxtimes D_3)\\right)=0. \\tag{4.13}\n\\]\nThe same holds for any closed subscheme with that support.</p>", "proof": "<p>If axis \\(i\\) at \\(g\\) meets axis \\(j\\) at \\(h\\neq g\\), their coordinates outside \\(\\{i,j\\}\\) agree, contradicting injectivity in at least one remaining coordinate.</p><p>Riemann–Roch gives \\(h^0(D_i)=2\\) and \\(h^0(D_i(-q))=1\\). Evaluation defines a degree-two morphism\n\\[\n\\phi_i:E_i\\to\\mathbb P(H^0(D_i)^*)\n\\]\nwith fibers of at most two distinct points. The marks \\((\\phi_1(g_1),\\phi_2(g_2),\\phi_3(g_3))\\) meet the hypothesis of Lemma 5.1. A section of the external product is a trilinear tensor; its vanishing on the three axes at \\(g\\) is precisely (4.11). Four such indices force the section to vanish.</p><p>The ideal of any closed subscheme with this support is contained in the radical ideal of the reduced union.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.3", "orig": "Lemma 4.4 (Finite models and arbitrary base change)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Let \\(B\\) be regular Noetherian of finite dimension, \\(f:V\\to B\\) projective, and \\(\\mathcal F\\) coherent and \\(B\\)-flat.", "statement": "<p>Let \\(B\\) be regular Noetherian of finite dimension, \\(f:V\\to B\\) projective, and \\(\\mathcal F\\) coherent and \\(B\\)-flat.</p><p>Then \\(Rf_*\\mathcal F\\) is locally represented by a bounded finite locally free complex. For every \\(h:T\\to B\\),\n\\[\nLh^*Rf_*\\mathcal F\n\\simeq Rf_{T,*}(\\mathcal F|_{V\\times_BT}), \\tag{4.14}\n\\]\nwhere restriction on the right is ordinary. Fiber cohomology dimensions are upper semicontinuous. If all derived fibers have cohomology in \\([c,e]\\), local models can be chosen in those degrees.</p>", "proof": "<p>Over \\(\\operatorname{Spec}R\\subset B\\), a finite affine cover of \\(V\\) has affine finite intersections. Its bounded Čech complex has \\(R\\)-flat terms, since relative flatness is checked at total-space stalks. Tensoring with any \\(R\\)-algebra gives the Čech complex of the ordinary pullback. The bounded flat complex is \\(K\\)-flat, proving arbitrary derived base change.</p><p>Proper coherence makes its cohomology finite over \\(R\\). Regularity and finite dimension give finite projective resolutions of these finite modules. Truncation triangles therefore make the derived object perfect; after localization it has a finite free model.</p><p>For such a model,\n\\[\nh^i(K^\\bullet\\otimes\\kappa(b))\n=\n\\operatorname{rank}K^i\n-\\operatorname{rank}(d^i_b)\n-\\operatorname{rank}(d^{i-1}_b).\n\\]\nMatrix ranks are lower semicontinuous, proving upper semicontinuity. Outside the desired amplitude, invert a maximal minor and split off a fiberwise injective first or surjective last differential. Repetition gives a model in \\([c,e]\\).</p><p>The perfect determinant\n\\[\n\\det K^\\bullet=\\bigotimes_i(\\det K^i)^{(-1)^i}\n\\]\nis independent of the model using canonical determinant trivializations of acyclic cones and commutes with base change. For an equal-rank two-term model \\(K^0\\to K^1\\), its determinant section lies in \\((\\det K^\\bullet)^{-1}\\) and vanishes exactly where the fiber complex is not acyclic.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 6", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "The fixed transform is the pushforward of an actual flat product-ideal kernel, with arbitrary derived base change.", "statement": "<p>The fixed kernel, coherent dual, and finite translation quotient.</p><p>Original labels incorporated: Propositions 4.6–4.8.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Fix the seed of Proposition 5.</p><p class=\"li\">Define\n  \\[\n  \\lambda(v)=t_v^*H\\otimes H^{-1},\\qquad a(v)=\\lambda(-v).\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\mathcal P\\) be the normalized Poincaré line on \\(X\\times\\widehat X\\), and put \\(M=X\\times\\widehat X\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Fix the equivalences\n  \\[\n  \\Psi(F)=R\\operatorname{pr}_{\\widehat X,*}\n  (\\operatorname{pr}_X^*F\\otimes\\mathcal P^{-1})[3],\n  \\quad\n  \\mu(x,y)=(x+y,y),\n  \\]\n  \\[\n  \\Phi=(1\\times\\Psi)\\mu^*,\n  \\qquad\n  \\widetilde\\Phi=\\Phi\\bigl((H\\boxtimes H)\\otimes-\\bigr).\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Set\n  \\[\n  I=I_\\Sigma\\boxtimes I_A,\\qquad G=\\widetilde\\Phi(I)[-3].\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The fixed transform is the pushforward of an actual flat product-ideal kernel, with arbitrary derived base change. Its shifted perfect dual\n\\[\nE=R\\mathcal Hom_M(G,\\mathcal O_M)[-1]\n\\]\nis an actual coherent perfect sheaf of rank \\(r=8d&gt;0\\), locally with a length-one locally free resolution.</p><p>For \\(g=(g_1,g_2)\\in G_1\\times G_2\\), put\n\\[\nb_g=(g_1-g_2,a(-g_1-g_2)),\n\\quad\nN_g=\\operatorname{pr}_X^*a(-g_1)\\otimes\n\\operatorname{pr}_{\\widehat X}^*\\mathcal P_{-g_2}.\n\\]\nThere are coherent unital associative line-translation actions\n\\[\nN_g\\otimes t_{b_g,*}G\\simeq G,\\qquad\nN_g^{-1}\\otimes t_{b_g,*}E\\simeq E.\n\\]\nThe map \\(g\\mapsto b_g\\) is injective. Its image \\(\\overline G\\) acts freely, and\n\\[\nq:M\\to Y_0=M/\\overline G\n\\]\nis a finite étale isogeny of abelian sixfolds.</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemmas 6.1–6.3 give the kernel and coherent dual.</p><p>For the coherent action, the invariant source ideals have their canonical \\(G_1\\times G_2\\)-translation linearizations. The normalized biextension identities give\n\\[\n\\Psi t_{v,*}\\simeq(\\mathcal P_{-v}\\otimes-)\\Psi,\\qquad\n\\Psi(L_\\ell\\otimes-)\\simeq t_{\\ell,*}\\Psi.\n\\]\nTensor conjugation by \\(H\\) gives\n\\[\n(H\\otimes-)t_{v,*}(H^{-1}\\otimes-)\n\\simeq(a(-v)\\otimes-)t_{v,*}.\n\\]\nThe shear satisfies\n\\[\n\\mu^*(a(u)\\boxtimes a(v))\\simeq a(u)\\boxtimes a(u+v),\n\\quad\n\\mu^{-1}t_{(g_1,g_2)}\\mu=t_{(g_1-g_2,g_2)}.\n\\]\nCombining these kernel identities yields exactly the stated \\(N_g\\) and \\(b_g\\).</p><p>Convolution with the fixed equivalence and inverse transports the genuine source compositors and their associativity. Graph kernels are line sheaves, so the transported degree-zero derived isomorphisms are actual line isomorphisms\n\\[\nN_g\\otimes t_{b_g,*}N_h\\simeq N_{g+h}.\n\\]\nChoices differ by constants because \\(H^0(M,\\mathcal O_M)^\\times=\\mathbb C^\\times\\). This obtains coherence from line kernels, not from simplicity of \\(G\\) or \\(E\\).</p><p>Perfect duality inverts line factors and commutes with translations. Dualizing and inverting the compatible maps gives the actual sheaf action on \\(E\\).</p><p>If \\(b_g=0\\), then \\(g_1=g_2\\) and \\(2g_1=0\\). Since \\(G_1\\cap G_2=0\\), \\(g=0\\). Nonzero translations have no fixed points. The quotient by this finite subgroup exists, is a finite étale torsor, and is a proper smooth connected group variety; over \\(\\mathbb C\\) it is projective, hence an abelian sixfold.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 6.1", "orig": "Lemma 4.5 (Local Tor and product ideals)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Let \\(D_1,D_2\\) be smooth curves in a smooth threefold \\(V\\), with finite or empty intersection.", "statement": "<p>Let \\(D_1,D_2\\) be smooth curves in a smooth threefold \\(V\\), with finite or empty intersection. Then\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Tor}^{\\mathcal O_V}_k(I_{D_1},I_{D_2})=0\\quad(k&gt;0),\n\\]\nand multiplication embeds \\(I_{D_1}\\otimes I_{D_2}\\) into \\(\\mathcal O_V\\), with image \\(I_{D_1}I_{D_2}\\). The quotient map to the reduced-union structure sheaf has zero-dimensional kernel. The assertions also hold for disjoint unions of smooth curves.</p>", "proof": "<p>At an intersection point choose a regular sequence \\(f_1,f_2\\) defining \\(D_1\\). Restricted to the regular one-dimensional local ring of \\(D_2\\), the left Koszul arrow is injective: at least one \\(\\bar f_i\\) is nonzero, and the ring is a domain. Hence\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Tor}_k(\\mathcal O_{D_1},\\mathcal O_{D_2})=0\n\\quad(k\\geq2).\n\\]\nThe two ideal sequences give\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Tor}_k(I_{D_1},I_{D_2})\n=\\operatorname{Tor}_{k+2}(\\mathcal O_{D_1},\\mathcal O_{D_2})=0\n\\]\nfor \\(k&gt;0\\), and \\(\\operatorname{Tor}_1(\\mathcal O_{D_1},I_{D_2})=0\\). Tensoring the first ideal sequence gives the injection and product image. Away from the finite intersection, product and reduced-union ideals agree. For disjoint unions, at most one component from each part occurs locally.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 6.2", "orig": "Proposition 4.6 (The relative kernel and all its base changes)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "On \\(X\\times M\\), with coordinates \\((y,x,N)\\), set \\( \\pi(y,x,N)=(x,N),\\qquad \\alpha_X(y,x,N)=y+x, \\) \\( \\mathcal I=\\operatorname{pr}_y^*I_A,\\qquad \\mathcal J=\\alpha_X^*I_\\Sigma, \\) …", "statement": "<p>On \\(X\\times M\\), with coordinates \\((y,x,N)\\), set\n\\[\n\\pi(y,x,N)=(x,N),\\qquad \\alpha_X(y,x,N)=y+x,\n\\]\n\\[\n\\mathcal I=\\operatorname{pr}_y^*I_A,\\qquad\n\\mathcal J=\\alpha_X^*I_\\Sigma,\n\\]\n\\[\n\\mathcal H=\n\\operatorname{pr}_y^*H\\otimes\\alpha_X^*H\n\\otimes\\operatorname{pr}_{y,N}^*\\mathcal P^{-1},\n\\quad\n\\mathcal F=\\mathcal H\\otimes(\\mathcal I\\otimes^L\\mathcal J).\n\\]</p><p>Then \\(\\mathcal I\\otimes^L\\mathcal J=\\mathcal I\\mathcal J\\subset\\mathcal O_{X\\times M}\\), and both this ideal and its quotient are \\(M\\)-flat. Ideal multiplication and ideal sequences commute with every base change. Moreover,\n\\[\nG=R\\pi_*\\mathcal F,\n\\]\nand for \\(h:T\\to M\\),\n\\[\nLh^*G\\simeq R\\pi_{T,*}(\\mathcal F|_{X\\times T}). \\tag{4.35}\n\\]\nAt \\(m=(x,N)\\),\n\\[\n\\mathcal F_m=I_AI_{t_{-x}\\Sigma}\\otimes\nH^{\\otimes2}a(-x)N^{-1}. \\tag{4.34}\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>The two subschemes are \\(M\\)-flat, the second being a translated copy of \\(\\Sigma\\times M\\), so their ideals are \\(M\\)-flat. Locally the first ideal has resolution\n\\[\n0\\to\\mathcal O\\to\\mathcal O^{\\oplus2}\\to\\mathcal I\\to0.\n\\]\nAfter tensoring with \\(\\mathcal J\\), its left arrow is injective on every fiber by Lemma 6.1.</p><p>Use the local flatness criterion [39, Tag 00MD]: for a local base map, a finite module mapping into a base-flat module, injectivity modulo the base maximal ideal gives injectivity and flat cokernel. Applied to \\(\\mathcal J\\to\\mathcal J^{\\oplus2}\\), it makes the tensor ordinary and \\(M\\)-flat. Applied to its multiplication map into \\(\\mathcal O_{X\\times M}\\), fiberwise injective by Lemma 6.1, it gives injectivity and \\(M\\)-flat quotient. These flat quotients make the exact sequences survive arbitrary base change.</p><p>Expanding the shear and Fourier–Mukai kernel gives \\(G=R\\pi_*\\mathcal F\\); the shift \\([-3]\\) cancels the kernel shift \\([3]\\). The fiber formula uses \\(t_x^*H=H\\otimes a(-x)\\). Since \\(M\\) is regular and \\(\\pi\\) projective, Lemma 5.3 gives the finite model and arbitrary base change.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 6.3", "orig": "Proposition 4.7 (The actual coherent dual sheaf)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Every derived fiber of \\(G\\) has cohomology only in degrees one and two, and degree-two cohomology vanishes on a nonempty open set.", "statement": "<p>Every derived fiber of \\(G\\) has cohomology only in degrees one and two, and degree-two cohomology vanishes on a nonempty open set. Consequently \\(E=R\\mathcal Hom(G,\\mathcal O_M)[-1]\\) is a coherent perfect sheaf with a local length-one locally free resolution and rank \\(8d\\). No reflexivity or codimension assertion is required.</p>", "proof": "<p>Write \\(I_{Z_x}=I_AI_{t_{-x}\\Sigma}\\) and \\(M_{x,N}=H^{\\otimes2}a(-x)N^{-1}\\). The exact sequence\n\\[\n0\\to\\mathcal F_m\\to M_{x,N}\\to\\mathcal O_{Z_x}M_{x,N}\\to0\n\\]\nhas ample middle line, with higher cohomology zero by Kodaira vanishing and\n\\[\nh^0(M_{x,N})=\\chi(M_{x,N})=\\int_X(2\\Theta)^3/3!=8.\n\\]\nThe seed vanishing gives \\(H^0(\\mathcal F_m)=0\\). Since \\(Z_x\\) has one-dimensional support,\n\\[\nH^3(\\mathcal F_m)=0,\\qquad\nH^2(\\mathcal F_m)=H^1(Z_x,M_{x,N}|_{Z_x}).\n\\]</p><p>The complement of the finitely many difference surfaces \\(\\Sigma_j-C_i\\) is nonempty. There \\(Z_x\\) is a disjoint union of \\(2n\\) genus-three curves, and the line has degree six on each. Curve duality gives zero \\(H^1\\), since \\(6&gt;2g-2=4\\).</p><p>Derived base change and Lemma 5.3 give a local model \\(K^1\\to K^2\\) for \\(G\\). Its map is generically surjective, so its dual is generically injective. The source is locally free and \\(M\\) integral, hence its kernel is zero. Dualizing and shifting yields\n\\[\n0\\to(K^2)^*\\to(K^1)^*\\to E\\to0.\n\\]\nThese local cokernels are the sole degree-zero cohomology of the globally defined dual, so they glue.</p><p>Each curve restriction has Euler characteristic \\(6+1-3=4\\). Thus\n\\[\n\\chi(\\mathcal F_m)=8-2n\\cdot4=-8d,\n\\]\nand the displayed resolution has rank \\(8d\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 7", "orig": "Proposition 5.1 (Hochschild–Atiyah trace identity)", "depth": 0, "snippet": "For \\(\\alpha=\\sum_{c+b=2}\\alpha^{c,b}\\), \\( \\operatorname{ob}_Q(\\alpha) =\\sum_{c+b=2}\\alpha^{c,b}\\lrcorner(a_Q^b/b!), \\) and on the full derived \\(\\operatorname{Ext}^2\\), \\( \\sigma_Q\\operatorname{ob}_Q=c_Q. \\) …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(A\\) be a complex abelian variety of dimension \\(a\\), \\(\\Delta:A\\hookrightarrow A\\times A\\), and \\(Q\\in\\operatorname{Perf}(A)\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Put \\(V=H^0(A,T_A)\\),\n  \\[\n  S_A=\\operatorname{Sym}(\\Omega_A^1[1])\n  =\\bigoplus_{b=0}^a\\Omega_A^b[b],\n  \\quad H_A=L\\Delta^*\\mathcal O_\\Delta,\n  \\]\n  \\[\n  HT^r(A)=\\bigoplus_{c+b=r}H^c(A,\\bigwedge^bT_A),\n  \\quad\n  H\\Omega_m(A)=\\bigoplus_{p-q=m}H^q(A,\\Omega_A^p).\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Use left interior multiplication\n  \\[\n  \\iota^L_{\\xi_1\\wedge\\cdots\\wedge\\xi_b}\n  =\\iota_{\\xi_1}\\cdots\\iota_{\\xi_b},\n  \\qquad s_b=(-1)^{b(b-1)/2}.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">With antiholomorphic cochains before holomorphic forms, set\n  \\[\n  (\\eta\\otimes v)\\lrcorner(\\omega\\otimes\\vartheta)\n  =(-1)^{bq}(\\eta\\wedge\\omega)\\otimes\\iota_v^L\\vartheta,\n  \\]\n  where \\(v\\in\\bigwedge^bV\\), \\(\\omega\\in H^q(\\mathcal O_A)\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Fix \\(df=f\\otimes1-1\\otimes f\\bmod I_\\Delta^2\\). Evaluation of the first infinitesimal diagonal defines the raw first-jet class \\(a_Q:Q\\to Q\\otimes\\Omega_A^1[1]\\). Powers append new forms on the right and then exterior-project.</p><p class=\"li\">The normalized bar map \\(I_A:H_A\\to S_A\\) sends\n  \\[\n  [f_1|\\cdots|f_b]\\longmapsto df_1\\wedge\\cdots\\wedge df_b/b!.\n  \\]\n  Define \\(HKR_A\\) by its derived adjoint and the graded dual pairing \\(s_b\\det\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let\n  \\[\n  \\operatorname{ch}_{H,j}(Q)=\\operatorname{Tr}(a_Q^j/j!),\n  \\quad\n  \\operatorname{ob}_Q=\\chi_QHKR_A:HT^2(A)\\to\\operatorname{Ext}^2(Q,Q),\n  \\]\n  \\[\n  \\sigma_{Q,j}(v)=\n  \\operatorname{Tr}_{Q,\\Omega_A^j[j+2]}\n  \\left((a_Q^j/j!)[2]\\circ v\\right),\n  \\quad\n  c_Q(\\alpha)=\\alpha\\lrcorner\\operatorname{ch}_H(Q).\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For \\(\\alpha=\\sum_{c+b=2}\\alpha^{c,b}\\),\n\\[\n\\operatorname{ob}_Q(\\alpha)\n=\\sum_{c+b=2}\\alpha^{c,b}\\lrcorner(a_Q^b/b!),\n\\]\nand on the full derived \\(\\operatorname{Ext}^2\\),\n\\[\n\\sigma_Q\\operatorname{ob}_Q=c_Q. \\tag{5.9}\n\\]\nComponentwise,\n\\[\n\\sigma_{Q,j}(\\operatorname{ob}_Q(\\alpha))\n=\n\\sum_{c+b=2}\n\\alpha^{c,b}\\lrcorner\\operatorname{ch}_{H,j+b}(Q)\n\\in H^{j+2}(A,\\Omega_A^j). \\tag{5.10}\n\\]\nUnder ordinary Dolbeault–de Rham comparison,\n\\[\n\\operatorname{ch}_{H,j}(Q)\\longmapsto\n(2\\pi i)^j\\operatorname{ch}_{B,j}(Q). \\tag{5.22}\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>For \\(\\beta:\\mathcal O_\\Delta\\to\\mathcal O_\\Delta[2]\\), put \\(a_A(\\beta)=L\\Delta^*\\beta\\). Naturality of the adjunction unit gives\n\\[\n\\Delta_*a_A(\\beta)\\eta_\\Delta=\\eta_\\Delta[2]\\beta.\n\\]\nEvaluating on \\(Q\\),\n\\[\n(1_Q\\otimes a_A(\\beta))AH_Q\n=AH_Q[2]\\chi_Q(\\beta). \\tag{5.23}\n\\]</p><p>Perfect partial trace is the coevaluation–braiding–evaluation composite and commutes with coefficient morphisms. Set\n\\[\nh_Q=\\operatorname{Tr}_{Q,H_A}(AH_Q),\\qquad\n\\sigma_Q^H(v)=\\operatorname{Tr}_{Q,H_A[2]}(AH_Q[2]v).\n\\]\nTracing (5.23) gives\n\\[\n\\sigma_Q^H(\\chi_Q(\\beta))=a_A(\\beta)h_Q.\n\\]\nThere is no odd exchange sign, since the degree is two.</p><p>Lemma 7.2 identifies \\(I_A(h_Q)\\) with \\(\\operatorname{ch}_H(Q)\\) and \\(I_A(\\sigma_Q^H(v))\\) with the stated \\(\\sigma_Q(v)\\). Lemma 7.1, with \\(\\beta=HKR_A(\\alpha)\\), therefore gives \\(\\sigma_Q\\operatorname{ob}_Q=c_Q\\).</p><p>Contraction of \\(\\operatorname{ch}_{H,j+b}\\in H^{j+b}(\\Omega_A^{j+b})\\) removes \\(b\\) holomorphic forms and adds cochain degree \\(c\\), landing in \\(H^{j+2}(\\Omega_A^j)\\). This gives (5.10); components with \\(j&gt;a-2\\) vanish. The factorials have already been accounted for, and there is no further scalar.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 7.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Normalized HKR action on the formal diagonal, from Subsection 5.1.3.", "statement": "<p>Normalized HKR action on the formal diagonal, from Subsection 5.1.3.</p><p>With the conventions of Proposition 7, \\(HKR_A:HT^\\bullet(A)\\to HH^\\bullet(A)\\) is an isomorphism. On hypercohomology,\n\\[\nI_A\\!\\left((L\\Delta^*HKR_A(\\alpha))h\\right)\n=\\alpha\\lrcorner I_A(h). \\tag{5.16}\n\\]\nIn unshifted differential-operator notation,\n\\[\nHKR_A(\\xi_1\\wedge\\cdots\\wedge\\xi_b)\n=\n\\frac{s_b}{b!}\\sum_{\\tau\\in S_b}\n\\operatorname{sgn}(\\tau)\\,\n\\xi_{\\tau(1)}\\otimes\\cdots\\otimes\\xi_{\\tau(b)}. \\tag{5.17}\n\\]\nTranslations act trivially on \\(HT^\\bullet(A)\\) and, through this map, on \\(HH^\\bullet(A)\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>The difference isomorphism \\((x,y)\\mapsto(y,x-y)\\) identifies the formal diagonal with \\(A\\times\\widehat A_0\\). In characteristic zero, integrate invariant formal one-forms to obtain formal logarithm coordinates \\(z_i\\), additive under the formal group law. Its completed structure sheaf is\n\\[\nR=\\mathcal O_A\\widehat\\otimes_{\\mathbb C}\\mathbb C[[z_1,\\ldots,z_a]].\n\\]\nThese are formal normal coordinates, not global functions cutting out the diagonal.</p><p>Completion computes the required diagonal-supported operations: for a finite free resolution \\(F\\to R_0/J\\), flat completion gives\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Hom}_{\\widehat R_0}(\\widehat R_0\\otimes F,R_0/J)\n=\n\\operatorname{Hom}_{R_0}(F,R_0/J),\n\\]\nalso with locally free diagonal coefficients, compatibly with composition and derived pullback.</p><p>Use the global formal Koszul resolution\n\\[\nK=(R\\otimes\\bigwedge^\\bullet V^*,\\,d_K=\\sum_i z_iC_i),\n\\qquad C_i=\\iota_{\\xi_i}.\n\\]\nIts diagonal pullback is \\(S_A\\), and its Hom into \\(\\mathcal O_A\\) is \\(\\mathcal O_A\\otimes\\bigwedge^\\bullet V\\), both with zero internal differential.</p><p>Compare it to the completed bar resolution by\n\\[\nF_b(e_{i_1}\\wedge\\cdots\\wedge e_{i_b})\n=\n\\sum_{\\tau\\in S_b}\\operatorname{sgn}(\\tau)\nz_{i_{\\tau(1)}}^{(1)}\\cdots z_{i_{\\tau(b)}}^{(b)}.\n\\]\nInterior faces identify successive columns and vanish; the last sets the last column to zero; expansion of the first face gives the Koszul differential. The augmented completed bar complex is flat and exact, its contracting homotopy being continuous. The normalized bar map sends each summand to \\(dz_{i_1}\\wedge\\cdots\\wedge dz_{i_b}/b!\\); the \\(b!\\) terms sum to that form. Thus the prescribed \\(I_A\\), not merely some splitting, is the identity on the diagonal Koszul model.</p><p>The closed lifts \\(C_v^L\\) give a dg-algebra quasi-isomorphism\n\\[\n\\mathcal O_A\\otimes\\bigwedge^\\bullet V\\to\\operatorname{End}_R(K).\n\\]\nAfter augmentation it is the basis isomorphism given by \\(s_b\\det\\); \\(\\operatorname{End}(K)\\to\\operatorname{Hom}(K,\\mathcal O_A)\\) is a quasi-isomorphism because \\(K\\) is bounded free.</p><p>Resolve only the resulting finite trivial diagonal bundles by Dolbeault complexes. For invariant representatives,\n\\[\nL_{\\eta,v}(\\omega\\otimes k)\n=(-1)^{b|\\omega|}(\\eta\\wedge\\omega)\\otimes C_v^L(k).\n\\]\nComposition gives the total-complex products, and diagonal tensoring gives the stated contraction. The adjunction-unit roof has denominator the Koszul augmentation and numerator diagonal projection, so these lifts represent exactly the adjoint definition of \\(HKR_A\\). This proves the formulas and the isomorphism.</p><p>Invariant Dolbeault representatives span the groups on a complex torus. The formal comparison and these representatives are translation-invariant, proving the translation assertion. The trivial tangent bundle gives \\(\\operatorname{td}(A)=\\sqrt{\\operatorname{td}(A)}=1\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 7.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Exponential first-jet identity, from Subsection 5.1.4.", "statement": "<p>Exponential first-jet identity, from Subsection 5.1.4.</p><p>Let \\(\\eta_\\Delta:\\mathcal O_\\Delta\\to\\Delta_*H_A\\) be the adjunction unit, and let\n\\[\nAH_Q:Q\\to Q\\otimes H_A\n\\]\nbe its evaluation. Then\n\\[\n\\Delta_*I_A\\circ\\eta_\\Delta\n=\\sum_{b=0}^a a_\\Delta^b/b!,\n\\qquad\n(1_Q\\otimes I_A)AH_Q=\\sum_{b=0}^a a_Q^b/b!. \\tag{5.19--5.20}\n\\]\nConsequently the evaluation formula in Proposition 7 holds.</p>", "proof": "<p>The first diagonal extension is represented on \\(K^{-1}\\) by \\(e_i\\mapsto dz_i\\), with sign checked by \\(e_i\\mapsto z_i\\) in \\(R/(z)^2\\). A closed lift is\n\\[\nA(k)=\\sum_iC_i(k)\\otimes dz_i.\n\\]\nSuccessive application with appended forms gives\n\\[\nA^{(b)}(k)=\n\\sum_{i_1,\\ldots,i_b}\nC_{i_b}\\cdots C_{i_1}(k)\\otimes\ndz_{i_1}\\wedge\\cdots\\wedge dz_{i_b}.\n\\]\nOn a degree-\\(b\\) monomial, the contraction and exterior permutation signs cancel, and augmentation gives \\(b!\\) times its differential form. Other Koszul degrees contribute zero. The normalized adjunction-unit component is the projection without this factorial. Completion comparison therefore gives the first identity on the original diagonal.</p><p>Evaluate the identity on \\(Q\\). Kernel evaluation commutes with splicing and exterior projection, including for perfect complexes and shifts. This gives the second identity and\n\\[\n\\chi_Q(HKR_A(\\alpha))\n=(1_Q\\otimes\\alpha)\\sum_ba_Q^b/b!.\n\\]\nThe component \\(\\alpha^{c,b}\\) acts only on \\(\\Omega_A^b[b]\\), giving the evaluation formula.</p><p>For a line bundle, the first jet class is the \\(d\\log\\) transition cocycle. Perfect trace is additive on exact triangles by supertrace on cones. The flag-bundle splitting argument and injective pullback in Hodge cohomology reduce vector bundles to lines; finite locally free resolutions treat all perfect complexes. The exponential sequence with kernel \\(2\\pi i\\mathbb Z\\) sends the raw first class to \\(2\\pi i\\,c_1^B\\), proving the weightwise comparison (5.22).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 8", "orig": "Proposition 5.2 (Transport of evaluation and contraction)", "depth": 0, "snippet": "There is an isomorphism, depending only on the enhanced equivalence, \\( \\Phi^H_{HH}:H\\Omega_{-2}(A)\\simeq H\\Omega_{-2}(B), \\) such that for every \\(Q\\in\\operatorname{Perf}(A)\\) …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\Phi=\\Phi_K:\\operatorname{Perf}(A)\\to\\operatorname{Perf}(B)\\) be a Fourier–Mukai equivalence between smooth projective abelian varieties.</p><p class=\"li\">Fix a perfect inverse kernel \\(L\\), enhanced inverse adjunction units and counits, and their triangle identities.</p><p class=\"li\">Kernel convolution is\n  \\[\n  R\\star P=Rp_{XZ,*}(Lp_{XY}^*P\\otimes^LLp_{YZ}^*R).\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Use the fixed HKR conventions of Proposition 7.</p><p class=\"li\">Conjugation \\(C_\\Phi(M)=K\\star M\\star L\\), with counit identification \\(t:C_\\Phi(\\mathcal O_{\\Delta_A})\\simeq\\mathcal O_{\\Delta_B}\\), defines \\(\\Phi^{HH}\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Put \\(\\Phi^{HT}=HKR_B^{-1}\\Phi^{HH}HKR_A\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is an isomorphism, depending only on the enhanced equivalence,\n\\[\n\\Phi^H_{HH}:H\\Omega_{-2}(A)\\simeq H\\Omega_{-2}(B),\n\\]\nsuch that for every \\(Q\\in\\operatorname{Perf}(A)\\),\n\\[\n\\Phi_{\\mathrm{Ext}}\\operatorname{ob}_Q\n=\\operatorname{ob}_{\\Phi Q}\\Phi^{HT},\n\\qquad\n\\Phi^H_{HH}c_Q=c_{\\Phi Q}\\Phi^{HT}. \\tag{5.27}\n\\]\nHence \\(\\Phi^{HT}\\) transports both kernels, and preserves the equality\n\\(\\ker\\operatorname{ob}_Q=\\ker c_Q\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>The first square follows from Lemma 8.1 and Proposition 7. Define\n\\[\n\\Phi^H_{HH}=I_B\\Phi_*^{HH}I_A^{-1}\n\\]\non \\(H\\Omega_{-2}\\), using the diagonal adjunction identifications.</p><p>By Proposition 7, \\(c_Q(\\alpha)\\) is the HKR-homological image of \\(HKR_A(\\alpha)\\cap h_Q\\). Lemma 8.1 transports this to\n\\[\n\\Phi^{HH}(HKR_A(\\alpha))\\cap h_{\\Phi Q}.\n\\]\nApplying \\(I_B\\) and Proposition 7 gives \\(c_{\\Phi Q}(\\Phi^{HT}\\alpha)\\). All comparison maps are isomorphisms, so their kernels are transported as asserted.</p><p>This does not assert naturality of \\(\\sigma_Q\\) on arbitrary Ext classes, nor identify this homological map with a separately normalized Betti transform.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 8.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Enhanced evaluation and Serre transport, from Subsections 5.2.1–5.2.2.", "statement": "<p>Enhanced evaluation and Serre transport, from Subsections 5.2.1–5.2.2.</p><p>In the setting of Proposition 8,\n\\[\n\\chi_{\\Phi Q}(\\Phi^{HH}\\beta)=\\Phi_{\\mathrm{Ext}}(\\chi_Q(\\beta)).\n\\]\nThere are isomorphisms \\(\\Phi_*^{HH}:HH_m(A)\\to HH_m(B)\\), for \\(m=0,-2\\), which preserve cap product by degree-two Hochschild cohomology and transport the categorical character of \\(Q\\) to that of \\(\\Phi Q\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Evaluation of \\(K\\star\\beta\\star L\\) on \\(\\Phi Q\\) contains adjacent \\(L\\star K\\); the fixed inverse adjunction and triangle identities cancel them. Enhanced associativity, including shift isomorphisms, gives the evaluation equality.</p><p>Write\n\\[\n\\Sigma_A=\\Delta_*(\\omega_A[\\dim A]),\\qquad\n\\Sigma_A^{-1}=\\Delta_*(\\omega_A^{-1}[-\\dim A]),\n\\]\n\\[\nHH_m(A)=\\operatorname{Hom}(\\Sigma_A^{-1}[m],\\mathcal O_\\Delta)\n\\simeq\\mathbb H^{-m}(A,H_A),\n\\]\n\\[\nT^m(A)=\\operatorname{Hom}(\\mathcal O_\\Delta,\\Sigma_A[m]).\n\\]\nSerre duality gives perfect pairings\n\\[\n\\langle h,\\delta\\rangle_{A,m}\n=\n\\operatorname{Tr}_{A\\times A,\\Sigma_A^{-1}[m]}(\\delta h).\n\\]</p><p>The left and right adjoint kernel formulas\n\\[\nK^R=K^\\vee\\otimes p_A^*\\omega_A[\\dim A],\\qquad\nK^L=K^\\vee\\otimes p_B^*\\omega_B[\\dim B]\n\\]\nand their fixed adjunctions give the trace-preserving Serre isomorphism\n\\[\n\\theta:K\\star\\Sigma_A\\simeq\\Sigma_B\\star K,\n\\quad\n\\operatorname{Tr}_{B,\\Phi P}(\\theta_P\\Phi f)=\\operatorname{Tr}_{A,P}(f).\n\\]\nLet \\(u:C_\\Phi(\\Sigma_A)\\simeq\\Sigma_B\\), and define\n\\[\n\\Psi^m(\\delta)=u[m]C_\\Phi(\\delta)t^{-1}.\n\\]\nAssociativity gives\n\\[\n\\Psi^0(\\delta[2]\\beta)=(\\Psi^{-2}\\delta)[2]\\Phi^{HH}(\\beta),\n\\quad\n(\\Psi^0\\delta)_{\\Phi Q}=\\theta_Q\\Phi(\\delta_Q).\n\\]\nDefine homological transport by\n\\[\n\\langle\\Phi_*^{HH}h,\\Psi^m\\delta\\rangle_{B,m}\n=\\langle h,\\delta\\rangle_{A,m}.\n\\]\nPerfectness makes it an isomorphism.</p><p>For \\(\\beta\\in HH^2\\), cap is \\(\\beta\\cap h=(\\beta h)[-2]\\). The shift is even, so\n\\[\n\\langle\\beta\\cap h,\\delta\\rangle_{A,-2}\n=\\langle h,\\delta[2]\\beta\\rangle_{A,0}.\n\\]\nThe preceding identities and nondegeneracy prove cap compatibility.</p><p>The trace \\(h_Q=\\operatorname{Tr}(AH_Q)\\) is characterized by\n\\[\n\\langle h_Q,\\delta\\rangle_{A,0}\n=\\operatorname{Tr}_{A,Q}(\\delta_Q).\n\\]\nThis follows from the trace-compatible diagonal adjunction and partial trace. The evaluation identity and trace-preserving Serre isomorphism therefore give \\(\\Phi_*^{HH}h_Q=h_{\\Phi Q}\\).</p><p>The source identification in homological transport is determined by Serre coherence, not merely by canceling inverse kernels. Testing on \\(\\operatorname{Perf}(\\mathbb C)\\) with \\(\\Phi=[s]\\), the trace of \\(\\operatorname{id}_{\\mathbb C[s]}\\) is \\((-1)^s\\). Thus homological transport on \\(HH_0\\) has that sign, in agreement with \\(\\operatorname{ch}_H(Q[s])=(-1)^s\\operatorname{ch}_H(Q)\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 9", "orig": "Proposition 5.8 (The exact rank comparison and the fixed transform)", "depth": 0, "snippet": "\\( \\operatorname{im}o_I =\\operatorname{Ext}^2_{X^2}(I,I)^{G_1\\times G_2}, \\qquad \\ker o_I=\\ker c_I^2, \\) and \\(\\sigma_I\\) is injective on this full invariant group.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the seed, canonical source linearization, fixed equivalence, \\(I,G,M,\\overline G\\) of Proposition 6.</p><p class=\"li\">Use the raw character, evaluation, contraction, and perfect-trace maps of Proposition 7.</p><p class=\"li\">Write \\(o_Q=\\operatorname{ob}_Q\\), \\(c_Q^2=c_Q\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\n\\operatorname{im}o_I\n=\\operatorname{Ext}^2_{X^2}(I,I)^{G_1\\times G_2},\n\\qquad\n\\ker o_I=\\ker c_I^2,\n\\]\nand \\(\\sigma_I\\) is injective on this full invariant group. Likewise,\n\\[\n\\operatorname{im}o_G=\\operatorname{Ext}^2_M(G,G)^{\\overline G},\n\\qquad\n\\ker o_G=\\ker c_G^2,\n\\]\nand \\(\\sigma_G\\) is injective on the full invariant derived obstruction group.</p>", "proof": "<p>Translations act trivially on \\(HT^2(X^2)\\) and \\(HH^2(X^2)\\), by Proposition 7. Naturality of evaluation under translation, identified by the canonical source linearization, gives\n\\[\n\\operatorname{im}o_I\\subset\nW_{\\mathrm{inv}}=\\operatorname{Ext}^2(I,I)^{G_1\\times G_2}.\n\\]\nProposition 7 and Lemmas 9.3, 9.5 give\n\\[\n48=\\operatorname{rank}c_I^2\n\\leq\\operatorname{rank}o_I\n\\leq\\dim W_{\\mathrm{inv}}=48.\n\\]\nThus the image is the full invariant group. The identity \\(\\sigma_Io_I=c_I^2\\), together with equality of ranks, gives equality of kernels. If \\(w=o_I(\\alpha)\\) and \\(\\sigma_I(w)=0\\), then \\(c_I^2(\\alpha)=0\\), hence \\(w=0\\).</p><p>Apply Proposition 8 to the fixed enhanced equivalence \\(Q\\mapsto\\widetilde\\Phi(Q)[-3]\\). It gives isomorphisms \\(u,v,\\gamma\\) satisfying\n\\[\nvo_I=o_Gu,\\qquad \\gamma c_I^2=c_G^2u.\n\\]\nThe target action was constructed by coherent conjugation through this same equivalence, so \\(v\\) identifies the source invariant group with \\(\\operatorname{Ext}^2(G,G)^{\\overline G}\\). The line factors cancel on derived endomorphisms. Image equality and kernel equality transport, and \\(\\sigma_Go_G=c_G^2\\) gives injectivity on the full target invariant group.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 9.1", "orig": "Lemma 5.3", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For an Abel curve \\(D\\subset X\\), meaning a translate of the Abel embedding or its inverse, \\( [D]_B=\\theta_B^2/2,\\qquad \\operatorname{ch}_B(\\mathcal O_D)=\\theta_B^2/2-\\theta_B^3/3. \\) Normal restriction gives an …", "statement": "<p>For an Abel curve \\(D\\subset X\\), meaning a translate of the Abel embedding or its inverse,\n\\[\n[D]_B=\\theta_B^2/2,\\qquad\n\\operatorname{ch}_B(\\mathcal O_D)=\\theta_B^2/2-\\theta_B^3/3.\n\\]\nNormal restriction gives an isomorphism\n\\[\nH^0(X,T_X)\\simeq H^0(D,N_{D/X}),\n\\]\nboth of dimension three. For a disjoint union \\(Z\\) of \\(n\\) Abel curves,\n\\[\n\\operatorname{ch}_B(I_Z)=1-\\frac n2\\theta_B^2+\\frac n3\\theta_B^3. \\tag{5.39}\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>The Abel map induces the identity on the Jacobian lattice. In a symplectic basis with \\(\\theta_B=\\sum_{i=1}^3e_i\\wedge f_i\\), pairing \\(\\theta_B^2/2\\) with each degree-two basis element gives integration of its Abel pullback. Poincaré duality proves the fundamental-class formula. Translation acts trivially, and inversion acts by \\(+1\\) on \\(H^4\\).</p><p>GRR for the inclusion \\(D\\hookrightarrow X\\), with \\(\\operatorname{td}(X)=1\\), gives\n\\[\n\\operatorname{ch}_B(\\mathcal O_D)\n=[D]_B+\\tfrac12\\deg T_D[\\mathrm{pt}]_B\n=[D]_B-2[\\mathrm{pt}]_B,\n\\]\nwhere \\([\\mathrm{pt}]_B=\\theta_B^3/6\\). Additivity gives the ideal formula.</p><p>For the original Abel curve,\n\\[\n0\\to T_C\\to\\mathcal O_C\\otimes H^1(C,\\mathcal O_C)\\to N_{C/X}\\to0.\n\\]\nThe dual of the first arrow is canonical evaluation. Serre duality identifies the dual of the resulting \\(H^1\\)-map with\n\\[\nH^0(K_C)\\otimes H^0(K_C)\\to H^0(K_C^2).\n\\]\nNonhyperellipticity makes \\(K_C\\) separate length-two subschemes, embedding \\(C\\) as a plane quartic. It lies in no conic, so restriction of quadrics is injective; both source symmetric square and target have dimension six. Multiplication is therefore onto. Since \\(H^0(T_C)=0\\), the long exact sequence identifies normal sections with constant translation fields. Translation and inversion transport this identification.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 9.2", "orig": "Lemma 5.4", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Let \\(\\Gamma\\subset X\\) have order \\(n\\), acting transitively on a disjoint union \\(Z=\\coprod_{j=1}^nD_j\\) of Abel curves.", "statement": "<p>Let \\(\\Gamma\\subset X\\) have order \\(n\\), acting transitively on a disjoint union \\(Z=\\coprod_{j=1}^nD_j\\) of Abel curves. Give \\(I_Z\\) its canonical linearization. Then, equivariantly,\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Ext}^1_X(I_Z,I_Z)\n\\simeq H^1(X,\\mathcal O_X)\\oplus\\bigoplus_{j=1}^nH^0(X,T_X).\n\\]\nThe first summand is trivial; the second has the regular component permutation action and identity action on translation fields. Consequently\n\\[\n\\dim\\operatorname{Ext}^1(I_Z,I_Z)^\\Gamma\n=\n\\dim\\operatorname{Ext}^2(I_Z,I_Z)^\\Gamma=6,\n\\qquad\n\\operatorname{Hom}(I_Z,I_Z)^\\Gamma=\\mathbb C.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Locally \\(I=(f,g)\\) has resolution\n\\[\n0\\to R\\xrightarrow{(-g,f)}R^2\\xrightarrow{(f,g)}I\\to0.\n\\]\nThe relation for an endomorphism forces multiplication by a scalar, so \\(\\mathcal Hom(I_Z,I_Z)=\\mathcal O_X\\). Applying Hom gives degree-one cokernel \\(I/I^2\\); under generator change the left generator contributes inverse determinant, so globally\n\\[\n\\mathcal Ext^1(I_Z,I_Z)\n=(I_Z/I_Z^2)\\otimes\\det(I_Z/I_Z^2)^{-1}\n\\simeq N_{Z/X},\n\\quad\n\\mathcal Ext^{q\\geq2}=0.\n\\]\nThe local-to-global sequence is\n\\[\n0\\to H^1(\\mathcal O_X)\\to\\operatorname{Ext}^1(I_Z,I_Z)\n\\xrightarrow eH^0(N_{Z/X})\\to H^2(\\mathcal O_X).\n\\]</p><p>Independently translate the \\(n\\) components on the open locus where they remain disjoint. The flat ideal family gives a tangent map from \\(\\bigoplus_jH^0(T_X)\\) into Ext. For a field \\(\\xi\\), moving generators \\(f-\\varepsilon\\xi(f),g-\\varepsilon\\xi(g)\\) gives local cocycle\n\\[\ng\\xi(f)-f\\xi(g)\\in I/I^2.\n\\]\nUnder the determinant identification this is normal restriction up to one fixed sign. Lemma 9.1 makes the composite with \\(e\\) an isomorphism, splitting the sequence. This construction is equivariant; translation fixes \\(H^1(\\mathcal O_X)\\) and \\(H^0(T_X)\\).</p><p>Serre duality on the threefold, using a translation-invariant holomorphic volume form, pairs Ext degrees one and two equivariantly. Finite-group averaging identifies dual invariants, giving dimension six in degree two. Hom is \\(H^0(\\mathcal O_X)=\\mathbb C\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 9.3", "orig": "Proposition 5.5", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For \\(I=I_\\Sigma\\boxtimes I_A\\), \\( \\dim_{\\mathbb C}\\operatorname{Ext}^2_{X^2}(I,I)^{G_1\\times G_2} =6+36+6=48. \\) This is the invariant subspace of the full derived Ext group.", "statement": "<p>For \\(I=I_\\Sigma\\boxtimes I_A\\),\n\\[\n\\dim_{\\mathbb C}\\operatorname{Ext}^2_{X^2}(I,I)^{G_1\\times G_2}\n=6+36+6=48.\n\\]\nThis is the invariant subspace of the full derived Ext group.</p>", "proof": "<p>Perfect Künneth gives\n\\[\nR\\operatorname{Hom}(I,I)\n\\simeq R\\operatorname{Hom}(I_\\Sigma,I_\\Sigma)\n\\otimes_{\\mathbb C}^L\nR\\operatorname{Hom}(I_A,I_A).\n\\]\nDegree two is the sum of Ext degrees \\((2,0),(1,1),(0,2)\\). Over \\(\\mathbb C\\) there are no further Tor terms. Independent averaging gives\n\\[\n(V_1\\otimes V_2)^{G_1\\times G_2}=V_1^{G_1}\\otimes V_2^{G_2}.\n\\]\nLemma 9.2 gives dimensions \\(6,6\\cdot6,6\\), retaining every local-to-global contribution.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 9.4", "orig": "Lemma 5.6", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For either orbit ideal \\(I_Z\\), put \\( f_n=\\operatorname{ch}_H(I_Z) =1-\\frac n2\\theta_H^2+\\frac n3\\theta_H^3, \\) where \\(\\theta_H\\mapsto(2\\pi i)\\theta_B\\).", "statement": "<p>For either orbit ideal \\(I_Z\\), put\n\\[\nf_n=\\operatorname{ch}_H(I_Z)\n=1-\\frac n2\\theta_H^2+\\frac n3\\theta_H^3,\n\\]\nwhere \\(\\theta_H\\mapsto(2\\pi i)\\theta_B\\). Extend contraction to\n\\[\nc_{I_Z}^j:HT^j(X)\\to H\\Omega_{-j}(X).\n\\]\nThen \\(c_{I_Z}^1\\) is injective of rank six and \\(c_{I_Z}^2\\) is surjective of rank six.</p>", "proof": "<p>Let \\(U=H^1(\\mathcal O_X)\\), \\(W=H^0(\\Omega_X^1)\\), \\(T=W^\\vee\\), all three-dimensional. The polarization gives an isomorphism\n\\[\nj:T\\to U,\\qquad j(\\xi)=\\xi\\lrcorner\\theta_H.\n\\]\nFor \\((\\eta,\\xi)\\in HT^1=U\\oplus T\\), the projections to \\(H^1(\\mathcal O_X)\\) and \\(H^2(\\Omega_X^1)\\) are\n\\[\n\\eta,\\qquad -n\\,j(\\xi)\\wedge\\theta_H.\n\\]\nThe map \\(v\\mapsto v\\wedge\\theta_H\\) on \\(U\\) is injective: choose \\(v=u_1\\) and \\(\\theta_H=\\sum_i u_i\\otimes w_i\\); terms with \\(i=2,3\\) are independent. Thus rank is six.</p><p>The target in degree two is\n\\[\nH\\Omega_{-2}(X)\n=\\bigwedge^2U\\oplus(\\bigwedge^3U\\otimes W),\n\\]\neach summand of dimension three. The \\(\\bigwedge^2U\\) source block maps identically to the first. The \\(U\\otimes T\\) block has no first component and maps to the second by\n\\[\n\\eta\\otimes\\xi\\mapsto-n\\,\\eta\\wedge j(\\xi)\\wedge\\theta_H.\n\\]\nTaking \\(\\eta,j(\\xi)\\) complementary to each \\(u_k\\) gives all basis vectors of \\(\\bigwedge^3U\\otimes W\\). These blocks already surject; the bivector block is not needed.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 9.5", "orig": "Lemma 5.7", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The contraction map \\( c_I^2:HT^2(X^2)\\to H\\Omega_{-2}(X^2) \\) has rank \\(48\\).", "statement": "<p>The contraction map\n\\[\nc_I^2:HT^2(X^2)\\to H\\Omega_{-2}(X^2)\n\\]\nhas rank \\(48\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Künneth decomposes the source into degrees \\((2,0),(1,1),(0,2)\\). Since the character is \\(f_n\\boxtimes f_n\\), these blocks have images\n\\[\n\\operatorname{im}c^2\\otimes\\mathbb C f_n,\\qquad\n\\operatorname{im}c^1\\otimes\\operatorname{im}c^1,\\qquad\n\\mathbb C f_n\\otimes\\operatorname{im}c^2,\n\\]\nof dimensions \\(6,36,6\\). They occupy different factorwise Hodge-imbalance summands \\((2,0),(1,1),(0,2)\\) in the direct target Künneth decomposition, so cannot cancel. The total rank is \\(48\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 10", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "The algebraic endomorphism \\( \\eta(u)(x,N)=\\bigl(a^{-1}(N),[-d]a(x)\\bigr) \\) defines a unital \\(K\\)-action.", "statement": "<p>The spin character and actual polarized quadratic seed.</p><p>Original results incorporated: Lemma 6.1 and Propositions 6.2–6.6.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use \\(M,E,r=8d\\) from Proposition 6.</p><p class=\"li\">Put \\(K=\\mathbb Q(u)\\), \\(u^2=-d\\), and\n  \\[\n  L=H^1(X,\\mathbb Q),\\quad U=L^\\vee=H^1(\\widehat X,\\mathbb Q),\\quad V=L\\oplus U.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">For oriented dual bases \\(e_i,f_i\\), \\(1\\leq i\\leq6\\), let\n  \\[\n  p=c_{1,B}(\\mathcal P)=\\sum_i e_i\\wedge f_i,\\qquad\n  Q(e_i,f_j)=\\delta_{ij},\\quad Q(e_i,e_j)=Q(f_i,f_j)=0.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\vartheta:U\\to L\\) be \\(y\\mapsto\\iota_y\\Theta\\), so \\(\\vartheta^t=-\\vartheta\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(J\\) be the pointwise spin stabilizer of the rational plane spanned over \\(K\\) by \\(e^{u\\Theta},e^{-u\\Theta}\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Define\n  \\[\n  \\kappa_M=\\exp(-c_{1,B}(E)/r)\\operatorname{ch}_B(E).\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The algebraic endomorphism\n\\[\n\\eta(u)(x,N)=\\bigl(a^{-1}(N),[-d]a(x)\\bigr)\n\\]\ndefines a unital \\(K\\)-action. The line\n\\[\nL_M=\\operatorname{pr}_X^*H^{\\otimes d}\\otimes\n\\operatorname{pr}_{\\widehat X}^*(a^{-1})^*H\n\\]\nis ample and compatible with this action. Its actual homological Hermitian form is hyperbolic of signature \\((3,3)\\).</p><p>The class \\(\\kappa_M\\) is rational and \\(J\\)-invariant, with\n\\[\n(\\kappa_M)_3=a_M\\theta_M^3+w_M,\\qquad\na_M\\in\\mathbb Q,\\quad0\\neq w_M\\in W_K(M).\n\\]\nAt every compatible marked complex structure for the fixed rational action and polarization, every component \\((\\kappa_M)_j\\) has type \\((j,j)\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemmas 10.1–10.5 establish the fixed character calculation, actual action, polarization, hyperbolicity, and exceptional component.</p><p>For the all-period assertion, let \\(C\\) be the coefficient matrix of \\(\\theta_M\\). Lemma 10.3 gives \\(C=-fQ^{-1}\\). For a cohomology automorphism \\(g\\) commuting with \\(f\\),\n\\[\ngCg^t=C\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad g^tQg=Q. \\tag{6.19}\n\\]\nLet \\(I\\) be a compatible complex structure on homology, \\(T=\\eta(u)_*\\), \\(h_t=\\cos t+\\sin t\\,I\\), and \\(g_t=h_t^t\\). Commutation with \\(T\\) makes \\(g_t\\) commute with \\(f\\). Polarization compatibility gives \\(I^tCI=C\\), hence \\(g_tCg_t^t=C\\). The path lies in \\(SO(Q)\\).</p><p>On \\(W_+\\), the circle weights have multiplicities three and three, so \\(\\det(g_t|_{W_+})=1\\). Lemma 10.4 identifies this determinant-one vector centralizer with the vector image of \\(J\\), whose lift fixing both spinors is \\((g,1)\\). Hence the actual Hodge circle lies in that image. A degree-\\(2j\\) circle-fixed class has only type \\((j,j)\\). Lemma 10.5 makes every component of \\(\\kappa_M\\) fixed, proving the assertion.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 10.1", "orig": "Lemma 6.1 (The fixed Fourier matrix)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Let \\(F_-\\) be the cohomological transform for the inverse Poincaré kernel and shift \\([3]\\):", "statement": "<p>Let \\(F_-\\) be the cohomological transform for the inverse Poincaré kernel and shift \\([3]\\):\n\\[\nF_-(\\alpha)=(-1)^3(\\pi_{\\widehat X})_*(e^{-p}\\pi_X^*\\alpha).\n\\]\nFor an ordered subset \\(P\\subset\\{1,\\ldots,6\\}\\), \\(k=|P|\\),\n\\[\nF_-(e_P)=(-1)^{k(k+3)/2}\\epsilon_{P,P^c}f_{P^c}.\n\\]\nThus \\(F_-\\) is a rational parity-preserving isomorphism.</p>", "proof": "<p>Only complementary indices in \\(e^{-p}\\) contribute to integration. Moving their \\(f\\)-factors past the \\(e\\)-factors gives exponent\n\\[\n3+(6-k)(7-k)/2.\n\\]\nIts difference from \\(k(k+3)/2\\) is \\(2(3+1)(3-k)\\), which is even. Each basis vector maps to a signed complementary basis vector.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 10.2", "orig": "Proposition 6.2 (Fixed Clifford identity)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Let \\(\\tau(e_P)=(-1)^{|P|(|P|-1)/2}e_P\\), and define \\( \\varphi=(1\\otimes F_-)\\mu^*,\\quad \\nu=(F_-\\otimes1)\\mu^*,\\quad D=F_-^{-1}\\otimes F_-, \\quad \\varphi'=\\varphi(1\\otimes\\tau)=D\\nu(1\\otimes\\tau). \\) All factor …", "statement": "<p>Let \\(\\tau(e_P)=(-1)^{|P|(|P|-1)/2}e_P\\), and define\n\\[\n\\varphi=(1\\otimes F_-)\\mu^*,\\quad\n\\nu=(F_-\\otimes1)\\mu^*,\\quad\nD=F_-^{-1}\\otimes F_-,\n\\quad\n\\varphi'=\\varphi(1\\otimes\\tau)=D\\nu(1\\otimes\\tau).\n\\]\nAll factor identifications use graded shuffles.</p><p>Use the Clifford relation \\(vw+wv=Q(v,w)\\), Fock action \\(c(e)=e\\wedge-\\), \\(c(f)=\\iota_f\\), and spin vector representation \\(\\rho\\). For \\(m_g=c(g)\\), put\n\\[\n\\rho'_g=\\varphi'(m_g\\otimes m_g)(\\varphi')^{-1}.\n\\]\nThen, for every algebraic spin element,\n\\[\n\\rho'_g=e^{p/2}\\bigwedge\\rho(g)e^{-p/2}\n=e^{(p-\\bigwedge^2\\rho(g)p)/2}\\bigwedge\\rho(g). \\tag{6.7}\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Wedge and contraction generate all matrix units of \\(\\bigwedge L\\): occupation projections select a monomial, after which generators remove and insert factors. Dimension comparison gives\n\\[\nC(V,Q)\\simeq\\operatorname{End}(\\bigwedge L),\n\\]\nwith even part the parity-preserving endomorphisms.</p><p>Put \\(F=f_1\\cdots f_6\\), and\n\\[\n\\phi(s\\otimes t)=sF\\tau(t).\n\\]\nIts action is\n\\[\nc(\\phi(s\\otimes t))(r)=(-1)^3[\\tau(t)\\wedge r]_{\\mathrm{top}}s.\n\\]\nThe top reversal pairing is perfect, and both wedge and contraction are self-adjoint for it. Clifford reversion is therefore adjunction; norm-one spin elements preserve the pairing. Thus \\(\\phi\\) identifies diagonal spin action with Clifford conjugation.</p><p>For a bilinear \\(B\\) satisfying \\(B(v,v)=Q(v,v)/2\\), set\n\\[\nc_B(v)=\\epsilon_v+\\iota_{B(v,-)},\\qquad \\psi_B(x)=c_B(x)1.\n\\]\nOrdered monomials show \\(\\psi_B\\) is triangular and invertible. Take\n\\[\nB_0(e_i,f_j)=\\delta_{ij},\\quad B_0(f_i,e_j)=0,\\quad\nB_s=Q/2,\\quad\nA_0=B_0-B_s=\\tfrac12\\sum_i e^i\\wedge f^i.\n\\]\nDirect monomial expansion gives\n\\[\n\\nu(1\\otimes\\tau)=\\psi_{B_0}\\phi.\n\\]\nFor \\(I'=P\\setminus I\\), \\(j=|J|\\), both sides on \\(e_P\\otimes e_J\\) equal\n\\[\n(-1)^{j(j-1)/2}\n\\sum_{I\\subset P}\n\\epsilon_{I',I}\\epsilon_{I,J}\n(-1)^{\\sum_{i\\in I'}i-|I'|}\nf_{(I')^c}\\wedge e_{I\\cup J}.\n\\]</p><p>For this finite exterior calculation use \\(\\iota_{\\alpha\\wedge\\beta}=\\iota_\\beta\\iota_\\alpha\\). The commutator\n\\[\n[\\iota_{\\alpha\\wedge\\beta},\\epsilon_v]\n=\\alpha(v)\\iota_\\beta-\\beta(v)\\iota_\\alpha\n\\]\ngives\n\\[\n\\psi_{B_0}=e^{\\iota_{A_0}}\\psi_{B_s}.\n\\]\nThe symmetric symbol \\(\\psi_{B_s}\\) is equivariant for Clifford conjugation and exterior vector action.</p><p>On \\(\\bigwedge(U\\oplus L)\\), use orientation \\(f_1\\cdots f_6e_1\\cdots e_6\\). The determinant pairing defines \\(PD_Q\\). Direct evaluation and Lemma 10.1 give, in degree \\(\\delta\\),\n\\[\nD=(-1)^{3+\\delta(\\delta+3)/2}PD_Q.\n\\]\nMoreover\n\\[\nPD_Q(\\iota_{A_0}\\eta)=A_0^\\sharp\\wedge PD_Q(\\eta),\n\\quad A_0^\\sharp=-p/2.\n\\]\nThe degree signs in \\(\\delta-2,\\delta\\) have ratio \\(-1\\), so\n\\[\nD\\iota_{A_0}D^{-1}=\\epsilon_{p/2}.\n\\]\nThe map \\(D\\) commutes with the exterior vector action, which preserves \\(Q\\), degree, and orientation. Consequently\n\\[\n\\varphi'=D\\psi_{B_0}\\phi=e^{p/2}D\\psi_{B_s}\\phi,\n\\]\nand the last factor intertwines diagonal spin action with \\(\\bigwedge\\rho\\). This proves the exact identity as rational algebraic representations.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 10.3", "orig": "Proposition 6.3 (The polarized quadratic seed)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The pullback of \\(\\eta(u)\\) on \\(V\\) is \\( f(w,y)=(d\\vartheta y,-\\vartheta^{-1}w),\\qquad f^2=-d. \\) Its eigenspaces are \\( W_+=\\{(-u\\vartheta y,y):y\\in U_K\\},\\qquad W_-=\\{(u\\vartheta y,y):y\\in U_K\\}, \\) with eigenvalues …", "statement": "<p>The pullback of \\(\\eta(u)\\) on \\(V\\) is\n\\[\nf(w,y)=(d\\vartheta y,-\\vartheta^{-1}w),\\qquad f^2=-d.\n\\]\nIts eigenspaces are\n\\[\nW_+=\\{(-u\\vartheta y,y):y\\in U_K\\},\\qquad\nW_-=\\{(u\\vartheta y,y):y\\in U_K\\},\n\\]\nwith eigenvalues \\(u,-u\\); these are the annihilators of \\(e^{u\\Theta},e^{-u\\Theta}\\).</p><p>The polarization and Hermitian assertions of Proposition 10 hold, and \\(\\theta_M\\) is fixed by the vector images of the ordered line stabilizer and of \\(J\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>The Fock formula \\(\\iota_y e^{u\\Theta}=u\\vartheta(y)\\wedge e^{u\\Theta}\\) gives the annihilators. Substitution gives eigenvalues and \\(f^2=-d\\), as well as \\(Q(fv,w)=-Q(v,fw)\\).</p><p>The biextension identity gives\n\\[\n\\lambda^*=\\vartheta,\\quad a^*=-\\vartheta,\\quad\n\\lambda_*=-\\vartheta,\\quad a_*=\\vartheta.\n\\]\nFor \\(s_a=1\\times a:X^2\\to M\\), the pullback matrix is\n\\[\nf_a=\\begin{pmatrix}0&amp;-d\\\\1&amp;0\\end{pmatrix},\n\\]\nwhose homology transpose is\n\\[\nT_a=\\begin{pmatrix}0&amp;1\\\\-d&amp;0\\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\nIt is induced by \\((x,z)\\mapsto(z,[-d]x)\\), so conjugation gives the claimed algebraic endomorphism and unital field action. Each eigengraph has three holomorphic and three antiholomorphic dimensions.</p><p>Form \\(\\Xi(v,w)=Q(fv,w)\\) and raise both indices with \\(Q\\). The resulting degree-two bivector has matrix\n\\[\nC=Q^{-1}f^t=-fQ^{-1}. \\tag{6.14}\n\\]\nIf \\(B_{ij}=\\Theta(f_i,f_j)\\), then \\(\\vartheta=-B\\). In \\(s_a\\)-coordinates direct multiplication gives\n\\[\ns_a^*\\xi=d\\Theta_1+\\Theta_2.\n\\]\nThus \\(\\xi=\\theta_M=c_{1,B}(L_M)\\), and the line is ample. Ordered spinor-line stabilizers preserve their annihilators and \\(Q\\), commute with \\(f\\), and hence fix this raised class.</p><p>On actual homology \\(U\\oplus U\\), the Riemann form is\n\\[\ne((v,w),(v',w'))=dE(v,v')+E(w,w'),\n\\]\nwhere \\(E\\) is the principal Riemann form of \\(H\\). With \\(D_0=\\operatorname{diag}(d,1)\\),\n\\[\nD_0^{-1}T_a^tD_0=-T_a,\\qquad T_a^tD_0T_a=dD_0.\n\\]\nTherefore \\(e(T_ax,y)=-e(x,T_ay)\\), giving compatibility with \\(K\\).</p><p>For\n\\[\nH_{\\mathrm{hom}}(x,y)=e(x,T_ay)+ue(x,y),\n\\]\nchoose a rational symplectic basis \\(v_1,\\ldots,v_6\\), with \\(E(v_{2i-1},v_{2i})=b_i\\). The vectors \\(s_i=(v_i,0)\\) form a \\(K\\)-basis and\n\\[\nH_{\\mathrm{hom}}(s_i,s_j)=duE(v_i,v_j).\n\\]\nIts Gram matrix is the sum of three blocks\n\\[\n\\begin{pmatrix}0&amp;dub_i\\\\-dub_i&amp;0\\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\nEach is a hyperbolic Hermitian plane. This proves hyperbolicity of the actual form and signature \\((3,3)\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 10.4", "orig": "Proposition 6.4 (Ordered spinor stabilizer)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Let \\(W=W_+\\).", "statement": "<p>Let \\(W=W_+\\). Under \\(V_K=W\\oplus W^*\\), the ordered line stabilizer is\n\\[\n\\widetilde L_K=\n\\{(g,t)\\in GL(W)\\times\\mathbb G_m:t^2=\\det g\\},\n\\qquad m(g,t)=t^{-1}\\bigwedge g,\n\\]\nand the pointwise stabilizer is \\(J_K=\\{(g,1):g\\in SL(W)\\}\\). Its two spinor characters are \\(t,t^{-1}\\).</p><p>For \\(0\\leq j\\leq6\\),\n\\[\nH^{2j}(M,\\mathbb Q)^J=\n\\begin{cases}\n\\mathbb Q\\theta_M^j,&amp;j\\neq3,\\\\\n\\mathbb Q\\theta_M^3\\oplus W_K(M),&amp;j=3,\n\\end{cases}\n\\qquad H^{\\mathrm{odd}}(M,\\mathbb Q)^J=0.\n\\]\nThe ordered line stabilizer acts on the two scalar Weil lines by \\(t^2,t^{-2}\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>The complementary annihilators are maximal isotropic and paired by \\(Q\\). On the Fock module \\(\\bigwedge W\\), their spinor lines are the top and vacuum lines. A vector stabilizer is \\(\\operatorname{diag}(g,g^{-T})\\), implemented by \\(\\bigwedge g\\). Its reversion norm is \\(\\det g\\), as follows from the top reversal pairing. Any other lift differs by a scalar, because the Clifford algebra is the full matrix algebra. Norm one gives \\(t^{-1}\\bigwedge g\\), \\(t^2=\\det g\\). The top and vacuum characters are \\(t,t^{-1}\\); fixing both vectors gives \\(t=1\\).</p><p>Decompose\n\\[\n\\bigwedge^kV_K=\\bigoplus_{a+b=k}\\bigwedge^aW\\otimes\\bigwedge^bW^*.\n\\]\nThe \\(SL(W)\\)-invariants are intertwiners between exterior powers. Diagonal torus weights of basis monomials agree only for identical subsets or the empty/full pair. Elementary unipotents connect all subsets of a fixed cardinality, proving irreducibility and scalar endomorphisms for \\(\\bigwedge^aW\\), \\(1\\leq a\\leq5\\). Thus invariant blocks are \\(a=b\\), \\((a,b)=(6,0),(0,6)\\), each one-dimensional.</p><p>The balanced block is generated by powers of \\(\\sum_iw_i\\wedge w_i^*\\), hence by powers of \\(\\theta_M\\). The other two lines are \\(\\det W,\\det W^*\\), with characters \\(t^2,t^{-2}\\). Scalar conjugation exchanges them, giving the rational Weil plane. In degree six their exterior summands differ from the \\((3,3)\\)-summand of \\(\\theta_M^3\\), so the sum is direct. Invariants commute with scalar extension, proving rational descent.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 10.5", "orig": "Proposition 6.5 (The exceptional component of the actual sheaf)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The normalized character \\(\\kappa_M\\) is rational, \\(J\\)-invariant, and has a nonzero rational Weil component in degree six.", "statement": "<p>The normalized character \\(\\kappa_M\\) is rational, \\(J\\)-invariant, and has a nonzero rational Weil component in degree six.</p>", "proof": "<p>The orbit ideals have character \\(f_{d+1}=1-(d+1)\\Theta^2/2+(d+1)\\Theta^3/3\\). Thus their theta twists have common character\n\\[\nv=e^\\Theta f_{d+1}\n=1+\\Theta-\\frac d2\\Theta^2-\\frac d6\\Theta^3\n=\\tfrac12[(1+u^{-1})\\lambda_++(1-u^{-1})\\lambda_-],\n\\]\nwhere \\(\\lambda_\\pm=e^{\\pm u\\Theta}\\). Reversal exchanges \\(\\lambda_\\pm\\).</p><p>Let \\(T=v\\otimes\\tau v\\). GRR and the fixed transform give\n\\[\n\\beta=\\operatorname{ch}_B\\Phi(I_\\Sigma(H)\\boxtimes I_A(H))\n=\\varphi'(T).\n\\]\nThe shifts and duality give \\(\\operatorname{ch}_B(E)=\\iota\\beta\\), where \\(\\iota\\) acts by \\((-1)^j\\) on degree \\(2j\\), and \\(\\beta_0=r\\).</p><p>Expansion gives\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nT={}&amp;\\frac{d+1}{4d}(\\lambda_+\\otimes\\lambda_++\\lambda_-\\otimes\\lambda_-)\\\\\n&amp;+\\frac{d-1}{4d}(\\lambda_+\\otimes\\lambda_-+\\lambda_-\\otimes\\lambda_+)\\\\\n&amp;+\\frac{u}{2d}(\\lambda_-\\otimes\\lambda_+-\\lambda_+\\otimes\\lambda_-).\n\\end{aligned} \\qquad(6.18)\n\\]\nIt is rational and \\(J\\)-fixed, but not \\(\\widetilde L_K\\)-fixed, because its nonzero square components have distinct nontrivial characters \\(t^2,t^{-2}\\).</p><p>For an even class \\(\\alpha\\) of nonzero rank define \\(N(\\alpha)=e^{-\\alpha_1/\\alpha_0}\\alpha\\). Then \\(N(e^b\\alpha)=N(\\alpha)\\), and normalization commutes with graded algebra automorphisms. Lemma 10.2 makes \\(N(\\beta)\\) invariant under the graded \\(J\\)-action.</p><p>If its degree-six Weil component were zero, Lemma 10.4 would put every component in \\(K[\\theta_M]\\), fixed by \\(\\widetilde L_K\\). The class \\(e^{-p/2}\\beta\\) is \\(J\\)-fixed, so its degree-two term \\(b=\\beta_1-rp/2\\) lies in \\(\\mathbb Q\\theta_M\\) and is also ordered-stabilizer-fixed. But\n\\[\ne^{-p/2}\\beta=e^{b/r}N(\\beta)\n\\]\nwould then be ordered-stabilizer-fixed, and Lemma 10.2 would make \\(T\\) fixed, contradicting (6.18).</p><p>Thus \\(N(\\beta)_3=a\\theta_M^3+w\\) with \\(0\\neq w\\in W_K(M)\\), rational by the rational direct sum. Since \\(\\kappa_M=\\iota N(\\beta)\\), duality negates both degree-six terms and preserves nonvanishing.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 11", "orig": "Proposition 7.2 (Determinant normalization and derived descent)", "depth": 0, "snippet": "There exist \\(\\alpha\\in H^2(Y_0,\\mu_r)\\), a coherent \\(\\alpha\\)-twisted sheaf \\(B\\) of rank \\(r\\) with \\(\\det B\\simeq\\mathcal O_{Y_0}\\), and an invertible \\(q^*\\alpha\\)-twisted line \\(\\mathcal L\\) on \\(M\\), such that …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use \\(q:M\\to Y_0\\), \\(G,E,r\\), and coherent line-twisted action from Proposition 6.</p><p class=\"li\">Use raw \\(a_P=-\\operatorname{At}^{BF}(P)\\) and\n  \\[\n  \\sigma_{P,j}(v)=\\operatorname{Tr}((a_P^j/j!)[2]v).\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Write\n  \\[\n  H^{-2}(V)=\\bigoplus_{j\\geq0}H^{j+2}(V,\\Omega_V^j).\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">For a twisted perfect complex on a finite-band gerbe, endomorphisms and their trace have weight zero and descend.</p><p class=\"li\">Put \\(\\lambda_H=a_{\\det E}/r=\\operatorname{tr}(a_E)/r\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There exist \\(\\alpha\\in H^2(Y_0,\\mu_r)\\), a coherent \\(\\alpha\\)-twisted sheaf \\(B\\) of rank \\(r\\) with \\(\\det B\\simeq\\mathcal O_{Y_0}\\), and an invertible \\(q^*\\alpha\\)-twisted line \\(\\mathcal L\\) on \\(M\\), such that\n\\[\nq^*B\\simeq E\\otimes\\mathcal L.\n\\]\nFor every integer \\(i\\),\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Ext}^i(B,B)\\simeq\\operatorname{Ext}^i(E,E)^{\\overline G},\n\\]\nalso on trace-free kernels. In degree two, with this isomorphism \\(\\phi\\),\n\\[\nq^*\\sigma_B(v)=e^{-\\lambda_H}\\sigma_E(\\phi v).\n\\]\nThus \\(B\\) is fully semiregular.</p><p>Its normalized Betti character is rational and satisfies\n\\[\nq^*\\operatorname{ch}_B(B)\n=e^{-c_1^B(E)/r}\\operatorname{ch}_B(E)=\\kappa_M.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Write the action as \\(\\mathcal L_h\\otimes t_h^*E\\simeq E\\), reindexing \\(h=-b_g\\). On an evenly covered good analytic cover of \\(Y_0\\), take all sheets and trivialize the transition lines. Transporting \\(E\\) gives sheaves \\(B_i\\) and maps \\(\\rho_{ij}:B_j\\to B_i\\) with scalar triple products.</p><p>Trivialize the perfect determinant lines, write \\(\\eta_{ij}=\\det\\rho_{ij}\\), and choose local \\(r\\)-th roots \\(\\widetilde\\eta_{ij}\\), compatible with inverses. Then \\(\\rho'_{ij}=\\widetilde\\eta_{ij}^{-1}\\rho_{ij}\\) have determinant one, and\n\\[\n\\rho'_{ij}\\rho'_{jk}=\\epsilon_{ijk}\\rho'_{ik},\n\\qquad \\epsilon_{ijk}^r=1.\n\\]\nAssociativity gives the Čech cocycle identity. These locally constant \\(\\mu_r\\)-values define \\(\\alpha\\) and \\(B\\); determinant trivializations glue. Coherence, rank, and perfectness are local.</p><p>On actual sheet overlaps the comparing deck transformation is identity. The normalized maps are scalar multiples \\(l_{ij}\\operatorname{id}_E\\), with multiplier \\(q^*\\epsilon_{ijk}\\); these define \\(\\mathcal L\\), giving \\(q^*B\\simeq E\\otimes\\mathcal L\\).</p><p>Let \\(C_B=R\\mathcal Hom(B,B)\\) be its weight-zero descent. Perfect derived pullback commutes with Hom and trace:\n\\[\nLf^*R\\mathcal Hom(P,Q')\n\\simeq R\\mathcal Hom(Lf^*P,Lf^*Q').\n\\]\nFor étale \\(q\\), line cancellation yields \\(q^*C_B\\simeq R\\mathcal Hom(E,E)\\) with the honest conjugation descent action. Finite covering pushforward is exact, and averaging splits invariants. Thus\n\\[\nR\\Gamma(Y_0,C_B)\\simeq R\\Gamma(M,q^*C_B)^{\\overline G}.\n\\]\nThis proves the full Ext assertion. The perfect unit and trace satisfy \\(\\operatorname{tr}u=r\\), so \\(u/r\\) splits trace, compatibly with pullback and line tensoring; this proves the trace-free assertion.</p><p>Because the gerbe band is finite, its cotangent target is that of \\(Y_0\\). Atiyah pullback and tensor rules give\n\\[\nq^*a_B=a_E+a_{\\mathcal L}\\operatorname{id},\n\\qquad\na_{\\mathcal L}=-a_{\\det E}/r=-\\lambda_H,\n\\]\nusing \\(\\det B=\\mathcal O\\). Expanding exponentials and taking trace gives the semiregularity square. Multiplication by \\(e^{-\\lambda_H}\\) is invertible on the finite target. Lemma 11.1 and Proposition 9 therefore prove full semiregularity.</p><p>The same rules give\n\\[\nq^*\\operatorname{ch}_H(B)=e^{-\\lambda_H}\\operatorname{ch}_H(E).\n\\]\nWeightwise division by \\((2\\pi i)^j\\) gives the Betti formula. Its right side is rational; applying \\((\\deg q)^{-1}q_*^B\\) proves rationality of \\(\\operatorname{ch}_B(B)\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 11.1", "orig": "Lemma 7.1", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Even transpose gives an isomorphism \\( d_2:\\operatorname{Ext}^2(G,G)^{\\overline G} \\simeq\\operatorname{Ext}^2(E,E)^{\\overline G}, \\) with \\( \\sigma_{E,j}(d_2v)=(-1)^{j+1}\\sigma_{G,j}(v). \\) Hence Proposition 9 implies …", "statement": "<p>Even transpose gives an isomorphism\n\\[\nd_2:\\operatorname{Ext}^2(G,G)^{\\overline G}\n\\simeq\\operatorname{Ext}^2(E,E)^{\\overline G},\n\\]\nwith\n\\[\n\\sigma_{E,j}(d_2v)=(-1)^{j+1}\\sigma_{G,j}(v). \\tag{7.3}\n\\]\nHence Proposition 9 implies full invariant injectivity for \\(E\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Perfect duality is an anti-equivalence, inverts the line factors, and preserves the coherent conjugation action. The canonical identification of \\(D(G)[-1]\\) with the coherent sheaf \\(E\\) is equivariant.</p><p>In a finite free model, the dual Atiyah class is the negative transpose. Transposition reverses both Ext-degree-one factors and one-form factors; the two reversal signs cancel. The degree-two operation is even. Supertrace is transpose-invariant, while shift \\([-1]\\) changes it by \\(-1\\). The \\(j\\) negative dual factors and this shift give \\((-1)^{j+1}\\). These constructions respect model changes, so the calculation glues. The resulting diagonal sign operator is invertible.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 12", "orig": "Proposition 7.3 (A relative projective bundle carrying an ordinary sheaf)", "depth": 0, "snippet": "There exist a holomorphic \\(\\mathbb P^{m-1}\\)-bundle \\(p:Z\\to Y\\), a determinant-trivial \\(\\alpha\\)-twisted rank-\\(m\\) bundle \\(Q_0\\) with \\(Z_0=\\mathbb P(Q_0)\\), and an inverse-twisted tautological line …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(a:Y\\to S\\) be a polarized holomorphic abelian scheme of relative dimension six over a sufficiently small contractible smooth analytic germ, with central fiber \\(Y_0\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Use \\(\\alpha,B,r\\) from Proposition 11.</p><p class=\"li\">Put \\(m=r^{12}\\), \\(N=6+m-1\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\kappa_B=\\sum_{k=0}^6\\kappa_{B,k}\\) be rational horizontal, of type \\((k,k)\\) in degree \\(2k\\), specializing to \\(\\operatorname{ch}_B(B)\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There exist a holomorphic \\(\\mathbb P^{m-1}\\)-bundle \\(p:Z\\to Y\\), a determinant-trivial \\(\\alpha\\)-twisted rank-\\(m\\) bundle \\(Q_0\\) with \\(Z_0=\\mathbb P(Q_0)\\), and an inverse-twisted tautological line \\(\\ell_0=\\mathcal O_{\\mathbb P(Q_0)}(1)\\).</p><p>The ordinary line \\(H=\\omega_{Z/Y}^{-1}\\) restricts centrally to \\(\\ell_0^m\\), and\n\\[\n\\zeta_{B,s}=c_1^B(H|_{Z_s})/m,\\qquad\n\\zeta_{H,s}=a_{H|_{Z_s}}/m\n\\]\nare normalized Betti and raw Hodge classes. The former is rational horizontal of type \\((1,1)\\) and has fiber degree one. Each \\(Z_s,p_s\\) is projective algebraic, and \\(a\\circ p\\) has a relative ample line.</p><p>The ordinary coherent sheaf \\(F_0=p_0^*B\\otimes\\ell_0\\) is fully semiregular, with\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Ext}^2(F_0,F_0)\\simeq\\operatorname{Ext}^2(B,B),\n\\quad\n\\sigma_{F_0}(p_0^*v)=e^{\\zeta_{H,0}}p_0^*\\sigma_B(v).\n\\]\nThe class \\(A_B=p^*\\kappa_Be^{\\zeta_B}\\) is rational horizontal, degreewise Hodge, and centrally equals \\(\\operatorname{ch}_B(F_0)\\). Finally,\n\\[\np_{s,*}^B\\!\\left(\\zeta_{B,s}^{m-1}e^{-\\zeta_{B,s}}A_B(s)\\right)=\\kappa_B(s). \\tag{7.17}\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Put \\(\\Lambda=H_1(Y_0,\\mathbb Z)\\simeq\\mathbb Z^{12}\\). The universal coefficient theorem identifies\n\\[\nH^2(Y_0,\\mu_r)=\\operatorname{Hom}(\\bigwedge^2\\Lambda,\\mu_r).\n\\]\nLet \\(b\\) represent \\(\\alpha\\), \\(G_r=\\Lambda/r\\Lambda\\), and in an ordered basis define\n\\[\nc(x,y)=\\prod_{i&lt;j}b(e_i,e_j)^{x_iy_j}.\n\\]\nThis bimultiplicative cocycle has commutator \\(b\\). On the \\(m=|G_r|\\)-dimensional space with basis \\(v_y\\), define\n\\[\nT_xv_y=c(x,y)v_{x+y}.\n\\]\nThen \\(T_xT_y=c(x,y)T_{x+y}\\), giving a projective representation.</p><p>The multiplication map \\([r]:Y\\to Y\\) is a finite étale \\(Y[r]\\)-torsor. Contractibility identifies \\(Y[r]\\) with \\(G_r\\). Quotient \\(Y\\times\\mathbb P^{m-1}\\) by translations and inverse projective action. Local torsor sections give the required projective bundle.</p><p>To retain the exact finite-band class, rescale lifts to determinant one. Their \\(\\mu_m\\)-multiplier has the same commutator \\(b\\). On the torus, evaluation on the bar cycle \\([e_i|e_j]-[e_j|e_i]\\) is this commutator, so the obstruction class is the image of \\(\\alpha\\) in \\(H^2(Y_0,\\mu_m)\\). Adjust by a \\(\\mu_m\\)-valued cochain to get exactly a chosen \\(\\mu_r\\)-cocycle, still with determinant one. These lifts define \\(Q_0\\).</p><p>With projective space parametrizing lines, \\(\\ell_0=\\mathcal O(1)\\) has inverse twist. The Euler sequence\n\\[\n0\\to\\mathcal O_{Z_0}\\to p_0^*Q_0\\otimes\\ell_0\\to T_{Z_0/Y_0}\\to0\n\\]\ngives \\(H|_{Z_0}=\\ell_0^m\\). Fiberwise \\(H=\\mathcal O(m)\\). The quotient is algebraic and projective on every fiber: tensor translates of an ample line on the projective covering product, use permutation linearization, and descend ampleness through the finite quotient. Relative ampleness of \\(H\\) over \\(Y\\), combined with a local representing ample polarization line on \\(Y/S\\), gives relative ampleness for the composite. The global class \\(c_1^B(H)/m\\) is horizontal and has the asserted Hodge type and fiber degree.</p><p>Twists cancel in \\(F_0\\). Perfect Hom pullback and line cancellation give\n\\[\nR\\mathcal Hom(F_0,F_0)\\simeq p_0^*C_B.\n\\]\nThe identity \\(Rp_{0,*}\\mathcal O_{Z_0}=\\mathcal O_{Y_0}\\) follows after the finite étale torsor trivialization from \\(H^{&gt;0}(\\mathbb P^{m-1},\\mathcal O)=0\\). The projection formula proves the full derived Ext comparison. Atiyah tensor rules give the semiregularity formula.</p><p>Pullback on the raw Hodge targets is injective: after Betti comparison, multiply by \\(\\zeta_B^{m-1}\\) and integrate, using \\(\\int_{\\mathbb P^{m-1}}c_1(\\mathcal O(1))^{m-1}=1\\). The exponential is invertible, so Proposition 11 proves semiregularity.</p><p>Pullback, products, and \\(\\zeta_B\\) preserve horizontal rational degreewise Hodge classes. Centrally the tensor character formula gives \\(A_B(0)=\\operatorname{ch}_B(F_0)\\); its raw degree-\\(k\\) edge is \\((2\\pi i)^kA_{B,k}\\). Finally the projection formula and fiber hyperplane degree give (7.17).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 13", "orig": "Theorem 7.4 (Deformation over a smooth projective analytic germ)", "depth": 0, "snippet": "After shrinking \\(S\\), there is a coherent \\(S\\)-flat sheaf \\(F\\) on \\(\\mathcal X\\), with \\(F|_{X_0}\\simeq F_0\\), such that \\( \\operatorname{ch}_{B,k}(F|_{X_s})=\\beta_{B,k}(s) \\quad(k\\in I). \\) These are rational …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(S=(S,0)\\) be a smooth complex analytic germ.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\pi:\\mathcal X\\to S\\) be a proper holomorphic submersion of relative dimension \\(n\\), with a holomorphic \\(\\pi\\)-ample line \\(L_{\\mathcal X}\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(F_0\\) be an ordinary coherent sheaf on \\(X_0\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(I\\subset\\{1,\\ldots,n\\}\\), and assume injectivity of the raw map\n  \\[\n  \\sigma_{I,0}:\\operatorname{Ext}^2(F_0,F_0)\n  \\to\\bigoplus_{k\\in I}H^{k+1}(X_0,\\Omega_{X_0}^{k-1}),\n  \\]\n  \\[\n  v\\mapsto\n  \\left(\n  \\operatorname{Tr}\\left(\n  (a_{F_0}^{k-1}/(k-1)!)[2]\\circ v\n  \\right)\\right)_{k\\in I},\n  \\qquad a=-\\operatorname{At}^{BF}.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Suppose rational flat sections \\(\\beta_{B,k}\\in R^{2k}\\pi_*\\mathbb Q\\), \\(k\\in I\\), satisfy\n  \\[\n  \\beta_{B,k}(0)=\\operatorname{ch}_{B,k}(F_0),\\qquad\n  \\beta_{B,k}\\in F^k(R^{2k}\\pi_*\\mathbb C\\otimes\\mathcal O_S).\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>After shrinking \\(S\\), there is a coherent \\(S\\)-flat sheaf \\(F\\) on \\(\\mathcal X\\), with \\(F|_{X_0}\\simeq F_0\\), such that\n\\[\n\\operatorname{ch}_{B,k}(F|_{X_s})=\\beta_{B,k}(s)\n\\quad(k\\in I).\n\\]\nThese are rational algebraic cycle classes. No rank, simplicity, stability, local-freeness, reflexivity, or fixed-support assumption is imposed.</p>", "proof": "<p>Starting with \\(E_0=F_0\\), Lemma 13.3 identifies the relative character of every infinitesimal lift with the edge of the prescribed flat class. The \\(F^k\\)-hypothesis therefore permits Lemma 13.2 at every extension \\(A_{\\nu+1}\\to A_\\nu\\), producing a compatible tower of flat coherent sheaves.</p><p>Choose \\(c\\) so \\(F_0(c)\\) is generated by \\(b\\) sections and \\(H^1(F_0(c))=0\\). Fix a central quotient\n\\[\nV_0=(L_{\\mathcal X,0}^{-c})^{\\oplus b}\\twoheadrightarrow F_0.\n\\]\nAt each step the kernel of \\(E_{\\nu+1}(c)\\to E_\\nu(c)\\) is \\(F_0(c)\\otimes J_\\nu\\), whose \\(H^1\\) vanishes. Lift the quotient-defining sections compatibly. Nakayama makes the lifted maps surjective, giving compatible quotients \\(V_\\nu\\twoheadrightarrow E_\\nu\\).</p><p>The relative analytic Quot theorem gives a finite-dimensional analytic germ \\(T\\) over \\(S\\) and a universal flat quotient. Compatible quotients define a formal section. Write\n\\[\n\\mathcal O_{T,0}=R\\{z_1,\\ldots,z_h\\}/(g_1,\\ldots,g_v).\n\\]\nThe formal section solves these convergent equations. Artin analytic approximation supplies an exact convergent solution congruent modulo \\(\\mathfrak m\\), hence an analytic section preserving the central quotient. Pulling back the universal quotient gives \\(F\\). It need not realize the whole chosen formal tower; the central fiber suffices.</p><p>The smooth total space makes \\(F\\) perfect, and \\(S\\)-flatness gives \\(Li_s^*F=F|_{X_s}\\). Near \\(X_0\\), relative Serre generation and the regular-local bound give a finite resolution by total holomorphic vector bundles after shrinking. Their alternating Betti character restricts to the fiber characters and is horizontal. Its central value is \\(\\operatorname{ch}_B(F_0)\\), so uniqueness of flat transport proves the claimed character identities.</p><p>Each fiber is projective, GAGA algebraizes the sheaf, and its finite locally free resolution identifies its topological character with the class of the rational algebraic Chern character.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 13.1", "orig": "Lemma 7.5", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Put \\(R=\\mathcal O_{S,0}=\\mathbb C\\{t_1,\\ldots,t_e\\}\\), \\(A_\\nu=R/\\mathfrak m^{\\nu+1}\\), \\(X_\\nu=\\mathcal X\\times_S\\operatorname{Specan}A_\\nu\\).", "statement": "<p>Put \\(R=\\mathcal O_{S,0}=\\mathbb C\\{t_1,\\ldots,t_e\\}\\), \\(A_\\nu=R/\\mathfrak m^{\\nu+1}\\), \\(X_\\nu=\\mathcal X\\times_S\\operatorname{Specan}A_\\nu\\).</p><p>If \\(E\\) is an \\(A_\\nu\\)-flat coherent sheaf with central fiber \\(F_0\\), it is absolutely perfect as an \\(\\mathcal O_{X_\\nu}\\)-complex, of local projective dimension at most \\(n\\). For a finite \\(A_\\nu\\)-module \\(W\\) killed by the maximal ideal,\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Ext}^2_{X_\\nu}(E,E\\otimes W)\n\\simeq\\operatorname{Ext}^2_{X_0}(F_0,F_0)\\otimes W,\n\\]\n\\[\nH^q(X_\\nu,\\Omega_{X_\\nu/A_\\nu}^j\\otimes W)\n\\simeq H^q(X_0,\\Omega_{X_0}^j)\\otimes W.\n\\]\nCoefficient semiregularity is \\(\\sigma_{I,0}\\otimes1_W\\), hence injective.</p>", "proof": "<p>At a stalk let \\(B=\\mathcal O_{X_\\nu,x}\\), \\(M=E_x\\). Both are base-flat, and \\(B_0=B\\otimes\\mathbb C\\) is regular local of dimension \\(n\\). A minimal free \\(B\\)-resolution remains exact and minimal after base reduction: flatness of \\(M\\) and its syzygies preserves exactness. The reduced resolution stops by degree \\(n\\); its ranks are the original ranks, so the original stops too.</p><p>For \\(i:X_0\\hookrightarrow X_\\nu\\), flatness gives \\(Li^*E=F_0\\) and\n\\[\nE\\otimes_{\\mathcal O_{X_\\nu}}^L(\\mathcal O_{X_\\nu}\\otimes W)\n=i_*(F_0\\otimes W).\n\\]\nPerfect duality and projection formula identify its derived Hom with\n\\[\ni_*(F_0^\\vee\\otimes^LF_0\\otimes W).\n\\]\nDerived global sections give the Ext identity. The differential coefficient sheaf is likewise a closed pushforward, giving the Hodge identity. Pullback of relative first jets and evaluation gives central Atiyah class and trace, so the coefficient map is exactly the central map tensored with \\(W\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 13.2", "orig": "Lemma 7.6", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Fix \\(\\nu\\), let \\(A'=A_{\\nu+1}\\), \\(A=A_\\nu\\), \\(J=\\mathfrak m^{\\nu+1}/\\mathfrak m^{\\nu+2}\\), and \\(X=X_\\nu\\hookrightarrow X'=X_{\\nu+1}\\).", "statement": "<p>Fix \\(\\nu\\), let \\(A'=A_{\\nu+1}\\), \\(A=A_\\nu\\), \\(J=\\mathfrak m^{\\nu+1}/\\mathfrak m^{\\nu+2}\\), and \\(X=X_\\nu\\hookrightarrow X'=X_{\\nu+1}\\).</p><p>Let \\(E\\) be \\(A\\)-flat coherent with central fiber \\(F_0\\). Suppose for each \\(k\\in I\\) its raw relative character is the restriction of the Hodge edge of a flat \\(F^k\\) de Rham class on \\(X'/A'\\). Then \\(E\\) admits a coherent \\(A'\\)-flat lift on \\(X'\\), with its prescribed restriction.</p>", "proof": "<p>Put \\(K=\\Omega^1_{A'/\\mathbb C}\\otimes_{A'}A\\). Then\n\\[\nK=\\Omega^1_{R/\\mathbb C}/\\mathfrak m^{\\nu+1}\\Omega^1_{R/\\mathbb C}\n\\simeq A^{\\oplus e},\n\\qquad d:J\\hookrightarrow K.\n\\]\nOn homogeneous terms, injectivity is Euler’s identity\n\\(\\sum_i t_i\\partial_if=(\\nu+1)f\\).</p><p>The relative ample line algebraizes the Artinian spaces and coherent sheaves by projective analytic GAGA. The model is projective smooth over the Artinian base. Proper-smooth Hodge degeneration and arbitrary base change give free Hodge modules. Applying base change to the split algebra \\(A\\oplus W\\) and taking its \\(W\\)-summand gives, for every finite \\(A\\)-module,\n\\[\nH^q(X,\\Omega_{X/A}^j\\otimes W)\n\\simeq H^q(X,\\Omega_{X/A}^j)\\otimes_AW. \\tag{7.24}\n\\]\nThus this coefficient functor is exact, and \\(d\\) stays injective on Hodge cohomology. GAGA compares the ordinary relative first jets, perfect traces, Ext groups, and Hodge targets used below.</p><p>Let\n\\[\n\\delta_X:L_{X/\\mathbb C}\\to L_{X/X'}\\to\nH^{-1}(L_{X/X'})[1]=J_X[1].\n\\]\nThe full relative cotangent complex is not asserted to equal \\(J_X[1]\\). Smoothness gives\n\\[\n0\\to\\mathcal O_X\\otimes_AK\\to\n\\Omega^1_{X'/\\mathbb C}\\otimes\\mathcal O_X\n\\to\\Omega^1_{X/A}\\to0.\n\\]\nThe conormal map factors through \\(1\\otimes d\\) and is injective. Mapping cotangent transitivity to the conormal triangle identifies truncation on \\(H^{-1}\\) with the identity. Exterior comultiplication therefore gives\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{ccc}\nL\\!\\bigwedge^kL_{X/\\mathbb C}&amp;\\xrightarrow{\\rho_k}&amp;\\Omega_{X/A}^k\\\\\n\\delta_{X,k}\\downarrow&amp;&amp;\\downarrow\\nabla'_k\\\\\n(\\Omega_{X/A}^{k-1}\\otimes J)[1]&amp;\\xrightarrow{1\\otimes d}&amp;\n(\\Omega_{X/A}^{k-1}\\otimes K)[1].\n\\end{array} \\tag{7.26}\n\\]</p><p>For the sheaf obstruction, \\(Q=E\\otimes_AJ=E\\otimes^LJ_X\\) is an ordinary sheaf by flatness. Put \\(\\mathcal K=Lj^*j_*E\\). Its top cohomology is \\(H^0=E\\), \\(H^{-1}=Q\\), so truncation gives\n\\[\nQ[1]\\to\\tau_{\\geq-1}\\mathcal K\\to E\\xrightarrow{b_E}Q[2].\n\\]\nAdjunction and the fact that \\(Q\\) is a sheaf give\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Ext}^1_{X'}(j_*E,j_*Q)\n\\to\\operatorname{Hom}(Q,Q)\\to\\operatorname{Ext}^2_X(E,Q).\n\\]\nThe last arrow sends \\(\\operatorname{id}_Q\\) to \\(b_E\\). An extension representing its preimage has Tor boundary equal to multiplication \\(J_X\\otimes E\\to E'\\). Hence \\(b_E=0\\) exactly when there is an extension\n\\[\n0\\to E\\otimes_AJ\\to E'\\to E\\to0\n\\]\nwith that multiplication identification.</p><p>To identify the obstruction, keep both actions on\n\\[\n\\mathcal C=\\mathcal O_X^{(1)}\\otimes_{\\mathcal O_{X'}}^L\\mathcal O_X^{(2)},\n\\quad\n\\mathcal I=\\operatorname{fib}(\\mathcal C\\to\\mathcal O_X).\n\\]\nUniversal derivation on action (2), oriented by\n\\(1\\otimes f-f\\otimes1\\mapsto df\\), gives \\(\\mathcal I\\to L_{X/X'}\\), inducing the identity on \\(H^{-1}=J_X\\). A local free dg-algebra resolution checks this on a degree-\\(-1\\) generator whose differential is \\(j_0\\in J_X\\). Tensoring with \\(E\\) and truncating gives the stated \\(b_E\\). For first jets,\n\\[\n\\jmath(pf)-\\jmath(p)f=-p\\,df,\n\\]\nso \\(\\jmath+\\nabla\\) is a module splitting, and its commutator represents \\(\\operatorname{At}^{BF}\\). Therefore\n\\[\nb_E=(1_E\\otimes\\delta_X)[1]\\operatorname{At}^{BF}(E),\n\\qquad\no_E:=-b_E=\\langle\\delta_X,a(E)\\rangle. \\tag{7.30}\n\\]</p><p>Using \\(L\\operatorname{Sym}^k(L[1])\\simeq(L\\bigwedge^kL)[k]\\), contraction differentiates each of \\(k\\) Atiyah factors. Graded cyclicity makes their traces equal; the output shift two is even. Thus\n\\[\n\\sigma_{k,J}(o_E)\n=\n\\delta_{X,k}\\left(\\operatorname{tr}(a_{X/\\mathbb C}(E)^k)/k!\\right). \\tag{7.31}\n\\]</p><p>The exterior boundary \\(\\nabla'_k\\) is the restricted Hodge-graded Gauss–Manin map: quotient absolute de Rham forms by those with at least two base differentials and filter by relative degree. Therefore it kills the restricted edge of a flat \\(F^k\\)-class. Applying (7.26) to the absolute character yields\n\\[\n(1\\otimes d)_*\\sigma_{k,J}(o_E)\n=\\nabla'_k\\operatorname{ch}^{\\mathrm{rel}}_{H,k}(E). \\tag{7.32}\n\\]\nThe right side vanishes by hypothesis. Coefficient injectivity from (7.24), and Lemma 13.1, imply \\(o_E=0\\).</p><p>The resulting extension is \\(A'\\)-flat by the square-zero flatness criterion: its quotient is \\(A\\)-flat and multiplication \\(J\\otimes_AE\\to E'\\) is injective. Algebraic construction followed by flat local analytification gives the required analytic lift.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 13.3", "orig": "Lemma 7.7", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For every \\(A_\\nu\\)-flat coherent lift \\(E_\\nu\\) of \\(F_0\\), ordinary flat de Rham comparison gives \\( H_{\\mathrm{dR}}^l(X_\\nu/A_\\nu) \\simeq H^l(X_0,\\mathbb C)\\otimes A_\\nu, \\) and, for \\(0\\leq k\\leq n\\) …", "statement": "<p>For every \\(A_\\nu\\)-flat coherent lift \\(E_\\nu\\) of \\(F_0\\), ordinary flat de Rham comparison gives\n\\[\nH_{\\mathrm{dR}}^l(X_\\nu/A_\\nu)\n\\simeq H^l(X_0,\\mathbb C)\\otimes A_\\nu,\n\\]\nand, for \\(0\\leq k\\leq n\\),\n\\[\n\\operatorname{ch}^{\\mathrm{rel}}_{H,k}(E_\\nu)\n=\n\\operatorname{edge}_k\\!\\left((2\\pi i)^k\n\\operatorname{ch}_{B,k}(F_0)\\otimes1\\right), \\tag{7.33}\n\\]\nwith the displayed constant class belonging to \\(F^k\\). This is independent of the lift.</p>", "proof": "<p>Rank treats \\(k=0\\). Projective Serre generation and the bound in Lemma 13.1 give a global finite vector-bundle resolution \\(V^\\bullet\\to E_\\nu\\).</p><p>Choose a differentiable trivialization of \\(\\mathcal X/S\\). Holomorphic \\(t\\)-jets of relative smooth forms define\n\\[\n\\Omega^\\bullet_{X_\\nu/A_\\nu}\\to\\mathcal A^\\bullet_{X_0}\\otimes A_\\nu,\n\\qquad\n\\omega\\mapsto\n\\sum_{|a|\\leq\\nu}\\frac{(\\partial_t^a\\omega)|_{t=\\bar t=0}}{a!}t^a.\n\\]\nLeibniz and fiber differentiation commute with jets. The fiber type projections also have jets, retaining integrability. Locally the resulting Dolbeault complex is the ordinary one with Artinian coefficients, up to an invertible formal smooth coordinate change; it is a fine resolution respecting the form filtration. Both total complexes resolve the constant sheaf \\(A_\\nu\\), and the map is identity on constants, so it realizes flat comparison.</p><p>A holomorphic bundle on \\(X_\\nu\\) becomes smoothly isomorphic to its central smooth bundle tensored with \\(A_\\nu\\): successively remove transition discrepancies using the fine sheaves of smooth endomorphisms with coefficients in \\(\\mathfrak m^a/\\mathfrak m^{a+1}\\).</p><p>Choose a smooth right-module connection \\(D=\\bar\\partial_V+\\nabla^{1,0}\\). Its curvature has no \\((0,2)\\)-part. In the stated convention \\(a=-\\operatorname{At}^{BF}\\) is represented by \\(-F_D^{1,1}\\). The closed form\n\\[\n(-1)^k\\operatorname{tr}(F_D^k)/k!\n\\]\nlies in \\(F^k\\), with edge the raw character. The Chern–Simons identity for \\(D_u=D_0+u(D_1-D_0)\\),\n\\[\n\\partial_u\\operatorname{tr}(F_{D_u}^k)/k!\n=\nd\\left(\\operatorname{tr}((D_1-D_0)F_{D_u}^{k-1})/(k-1)!\\right),\n\\]\nproves connection independence over the Artinian algebra. Its flat class is \\((2\\pi i)^k\\operatorname{ch}_{B,k}(V_0)\\).</p><p>Apply this termwise to \\(V^\\bullet\\). Terms involving the internal differential have positive internal degree and zero supertrace; the remainder is the alternating bundle calculation. Central restriction resolves \\(F_0\\), proving (7.33).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 14", "orig": "Theorem 3.1 (Algebraicity of the hyperbolic Weil plane)", "depth": 0, "snippet": "\\( W_E(A)\\subset\\operatorname{im}\\!\\left(CH^3(A)_{\\mathbb Q}\\to H^6(A,\\mathbb Q)\\right). \\) The assertion holds at every compatible period, including nonsimple varieties and periods with additional endomorphisms.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(E\\) be imaginary quadratic and \\(A\\) a complex abelian sixfold with a unital \\(E\\)-action.</p><p class=\"li\">Assume a compatible polarization has actual homological Hermitian form hyperbolic of signature \\((3,3)\\).</p><p class=\"li\">For the two embeddings \\(\\sigma,\\bar\\sigma:E\\hookrightarrow k\\), define the rational Weil plane \\(W_E(A)\\subset H^6(A,\\mathbb Q)\\) by\n  \\[\n  W_E(A)\\otimes k=\n  \\bigwedge^6H^1(A,k)_\\sigma\n  \\oplus\n  \\bigwedge^6H^1(A,k)_{\\bar\\sigma}.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\nW_E(A)\\subset\\operatorname{im}\\!\\left(CH^3(A)_{\\mathbb Q}\\to H^6(A,\\mathbb Q)\\right).\n\\]\nThe assertion holds at every compatible period, including nonsimple varieties and periods with additional endomorphisms.</p>", "proof": "<p>Every imaginary quadratic field is \\(\\mathbb Q(u_0)\\), \\(u_0^2=-d_0\\), with positive squarefree \\(d_0\\). Set \\(u=2u_0\\), so \\(d=4d_0\\geq4\\) lies in the seed range. For the same compatible Riemann form,\n\\[\nh_u=2h_{u_0}.\n\\]\nScaling does not change isotropic subspaces or balanced signature. Lemma 14.5 therefore applies to every field and every compatible hyperbolic period, proving the proposition.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 14.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Marked compatible domain and families, from Subsection 8.1.", "statement": "<p>Marked compatible domain and families, from Subsection 8.1.</p><p>Fix \\(K=\\mathbb Q(u)\\), \\(u^2=-d\\), \\(d\\geq3\\), and the quotient seed \\(Y_0\\) from Proposition 11. Transport the action and polarization through \\(q\\). Put\n\\[\nV_0=H_1(Y_0,\\mathbb Q),\\quad \\Lambda_0=H_1(Y_0,\\mathbb Z),\n\\quad \\theta_0=(q^*)^{-1}\\theta_M,\n\\]\nand let \\(e_0,T_0\\) be the corresponding Riemann form and action of \\(u\\).</p><p>The domain\n\\[\n\\mathcal D=\\{I:I^2=-1,\\ IT_0=T_0I,\\ e_0(Ix,Iy)=e_0(x,y),\\\ne_0(x,Ix)&gt;0\\ (x\\neq0)\\}\n\\]\nis a connected contractible complex manifold of dimension nine. It supports a marked polarized holomorphic family \\(Y_I=(V_{0,\\mathbb R},I)/\\Lambda_0\\).</p><p>There is also a principal lattice \\(\\Lambda_{\\mathrm{pp}}\\), a holomorphic map\n\\[\n\\nu:\\mathcal D\\to\\mathcal A_6(4)^{an}\n\\]\nto the smooth quasi-projective fine level-four moduli variety, and fiberwise algebraic \\(K\\)-linear isogenies \\(A_I^{\\mathrm{pp}}\\to Y_I\\), where the principal family is the analytic pullback of the smooth projective universal abelian scheme.</p><p>The class \\(\\kappa_B=\\operatorname{ch}_B(B)\\) transports horizontally over \\(\\mathcal D\\), is degreewise of type \\((j,j)\\), and\n\\[\n\\kappa_{B,3}(I)=c_\\theta\\theta_I^3+w_I,\\qquad\n0\\neq w_I\\in W_K(Y_I).\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>The actual quotient Hermitian form\n\\[\nh_0(x,y)=e_0(x,T_0y)+ue_0(x,y)\n\\]\nis isometric through \\(q_*\\) to the hyperbolic seed form.</p><p>For the embedding \\(\\sigma(u)=i\\sqrt d\\), compatible \\(I\\) corresponds exactly to a positive three-plane \\(P\\) in the Hermitian \\((3,3)\\)-space, with \\(I=i\\) on \\(P\\), \\(-i\\) on \\(P^\\perp\\). Positivity follows from\n\\[\ne_0(x,Ix)=d^{-1/2}\n\\bigl(h_{0,\\sigma}(x_+,x_+)-h_{0,\\sigma}(x_-,x_-)\\bigr).\n\\]\nRelative to a fixed positive/negative splitting, \\(P\\) is the graph of \\(Z\\) with \\(1-Z^*Z&gt;0\\), equivalently \\(\\|Z\\|_{\\mathrm{op}}&lt;1\\). This ball is convex.</p><p>For the holomorphic convention, in cohomological eigenspaces \\(W_\\sigma,W_{\\bar\\sigma}\\),\n\\[\nF^1=P^\\vee\\oplus\\operatorname{Ann}(P^\\vee).\n\\]\nThe annihilator map is algebraic, so this defines a holomorphic map into the Siegel domain and gives \\(\\mathcal D\\) its complex structure.</p><p>Choose a rational symplectic basis of \\(e_0\\) with pairings one, and let \\(\\Lambda_{\\mathrm{pp}}\\) be its integral span. It is unimodular, so all associated tori are principally polarized. Rational \\(K\\)-operators persist in \\(\\operatorname{End}^0\\). The fine symplectic level-four theorem gives \\(\\mathcal A_6(4)\\), its universal family, and marked analytic uniformization. The universal total space is quasi-projective; its proper locally closed graph embedding into projective space over the base is closed, proving relative projectivity.</p><p>Choose \\(c_{\\mathrm{lat}}&gt;0\\) with \\(c_{\\mathrm{lat}}\\Lambda_{\\mathrm{pp}}\\subset\\Lambda_0\\). The finite constant torsion group\n\\[\nc_{\\mathrm{lat}}^{-1}\\Lambda_0/\\Lambda_{\\mathrm{pp}}\n\\]\ngives holomorphic torsion sections over the contractible domain. Quotient the principal family and multiply its lattice by \\(c_{\\mathrm{lat}}\\) to obtain exactly \\(Y_I\\). The corresponding fiberwise isogenies are algebraic by projective GAGA.</p><p>Choose \\(c_{\\mathrm{pol}}&gt;0\\) making \\(c_{\\mathrm{pol}}e_0\\) integral on \\(\\Lambda_0\\). In the dual lattice marking, the map\n\\[\nx\\mapsto[y\\mapsto c_{\\mathrm{pol}}e_0(y,x)]\n\\]\nis complex-linear from \\(I\\) to \\(-I^t\\), hence defines a holomorphic family homomorphism to the dual. Fiberwise it is the Riemann polarization. A local relative Picard section gives a representing line and relative ampleness near the seed.</p><p>Proposition 11 gives \\(q^*\\kappa_B=\\kappa_M\\), rationally. The constant \\(q^*\\) identifies rational cohomology, eigenspaces, and Weil planes at corresponding marked structures. Proposition 10 therefore gives all Hodge-type and nonzero Weil-component assertions.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 14.2", "orig": "Proposition 8.1 (Local algebraicity of the descended character)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "On a nonempty analytic neighborhood of the seed in \\(\\mathcal D\\), every \\(\\kappa_{B,j}(I)\\) is a rational algebraic cycle class on \\(Y_I\\).", "statement": "<p>On a nonempty analytic neighborhood of the seed in \\(\\mathcal D\\), every \\(\\kappa_{B,j}(I)\\) is a rational algebraic cycle class on \\(Y_I\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Restrict Lemma 14.1 to a small contractible smooth nine-dimensional germ. Apply Proposition 12 to \\(B\\), obtaining \\(p:Z\\to Y\\), the ordinary fully semiregular \\(F_0\\), and horizontal hyperplane class \\(\\zeta_B\\). Put\n\\[\nA_B=p^*\\kappa_Be^{\\zeta_B}.\n\\]\nIts degree-\\(2k\\) components are rational flat of type \\((k,k)\\), centrally \\(\\operatorname{ch}_{B,k}(F_0)\\). Their raw edges are \\((2\\pi i)^kA_{B,k}\\).</p><p>Apply Proposition 13 with \\(I=\\{1,\\ldots,N\\}\\), \\(N=6+m-1\\). This retains every possible semiregularity target \\(H^{j+2}(\\Omega^j)\\), at \\(k=j+1\\). All \\(A_{B,k}\\) become algebraic on one smaller neighborhood; the degree-zero rank already is algebraic.</p><p>Represent \\(\\zeta_B\\) in Chow by \\(c_1^{CH}(H)/m\\). Multiply algebraic representatives by the finite polynomial \\(\\zeta_B^{m-1}e^{-\\zeta_B}\\), and push forward along the algebraic \\(p_s\\). Proposition 12 identifies the resulting Betti class with \\(\\kappa_B(s)\\). Homogeneous components prove the assertion.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 14.3", "orig": "Lemma 8.2", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Let \\(A\\) be an abelian sixfold with a unital imaginary-quadratic \\(K\\)-action.", "statement": "<p>Let \\(A\\) be an abelian sixfold with a unital imaginary-quadratic \\(K\\)-action. If one nonzero rational vector in \\(W_K(A)\\) is algebraic, the entire rational plane is algebraic.</p>", "proof": "<p>Choose \\(k_0=1+tu\\in K^\\times\\), \\(t\\in\\mathbb Q\\), such that \\(\\sigma(k_0)^6\\neq\\bar\\sigma(k_0)^6\\). Only finitely many \\(t\\) are excluded, because\n\\[\n(1+tu)^6-(1-tu)^6\n\\]\nis a nonzero polynomial with linear coefficient \\(12u\\).</p><p>A nonzero rational \\(w\\) has nonzero coordinates in both conjugate determinant lines. The pullback \\(k_0^*\\) acts there by distinct eigenvalues, so \\(w,k_0^*w\\) are rationally independent. Clear denominators in the rational endomorphism: if \\(h=nk_0\\) is integral, then on \\(H^6\\), \\(k_0^*=n^{-6}h^*\\). Thus both basis vectors are algebraic.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 14.4", "orig": "Proposition 8.3 (Analytic Hilbert spread)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Let \\(\\pi:\\mathcal X\\to S\\) be smooth projective of relative dimension \\(n\\), with \\(S\\) smooth quasi-projective over \\(\\mathbb C\\).", "statement": "<p>Let \\(\\pi:\\mathcal X\\to S\\) be smooth projective of relative dimension \\(n\\), with \\(S\\) smooth quasi-projective over \\(\\mathbb C\\). Let \\(\\nu:T\\to S^{an}\\) be holomorphic, with \\(T\\) connected complex manifold. Fix \\(0\\leq c\\leq n\\) and a finite-rank rational local subsystem\n\\[\n\\mathcal V\\subset\\nu^*R^{2c}\\pi_*^{an}\\mathbb Q.\n\\]\nIf every vector of \\(\\mathcal V_t\\) is algebraic on a nonempty analytic open of \\(T\\), the same holds for every \\(t\\in T\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Let the rank be \\(b&gt;0\\); rank zero is immediate. Choose a relative very ample line. For \\(b\\) candidate generators choose finite lists of rational coefficients and Hilbert polynomials of degree \\(n-c\\). There are countably many choices. Each gives a product \\(H\\to S\\) of projective relative Hilbert schemes with universal flat subschemes.</p><p>Their structure sheaves on \\(\\mathcal X_H\\) are absolutely perfect with local projective dimension at most \\(n\\). Indeed, at \\(x\\mapsto h\\), write \\(R=\\mathcal O_{H,h}\\), \\(B=\\mathcal O_{\\mathcal X_H,x}\\), \\(M=\\mathcal F_x\\). Both \\(B,M\\) are \\(R\\)-flat and the fiber ring \\(\\bar B\\) is regular of dimension at most \\(n\\). Derived associativity gives\n\\[\nM\\otimes_B^Lk(x)\n\\simeq(M\\otimes_Rk(h))\\otimes_{\\bar B}^Lk(x),\n\\]\nwhose Tor vanishes above \\(n\\). A minimal \\(B\\)-resolution therefore stops there. Quasi-projectivity and the ample-line resolution property give a finite global vector-bundle resolution, restricting exactly to fibers by flatness.</p><p>Cycle classes are locally constant even at singular or nonreduced Hilbert points. Trivialize the original smooth family differentiably over a coordinate ball in \\(S^{an}\\), then pull the product back to \\(H^{an}\\). A vector bundle on a compact fiber times a neighborhood is embedded into a fixed trivial bundle using finitely many trivializations and a partition of unity on the fiber. Its continuous orthogonal projections \\(P(x,h)\\) satisfy, after shrinking,\n\\[\n\\|P(x,h)-P(x,h_0)\\|&lt;1.\n\\]\nProjection identifies the fiber bundles, proving local constancy of their characters, hence of the alternating character of the universal structure sheaf.</p><p>To identify the fundamental cycle with scheme multiplicities, use Baum–Fulton–MacPherson Riemann–Roch. For \\(\\mathcal F_h\\) supported in dimension at most \\(n-c\\), devissage gives its top support term \\(\\sum_i\\ell_i[\\mathcal O_{V_i}]\\), with generic lengths \\(\\ell_i\\). Since \\(\\tau(\\mathcal F_h)=\\operatorname{ch}^{CH}(\\mathcal F_h)\\operatorname{td}(T_{X_s})\\cap[X_s]\\) and \\(\\operatorname{td}_0=1\\),\n\\[\n\\operatorname{ch}_{B,c}(\\mathcal O_{Z_h})\n=\\operatorname{cl}_B([Z_h]_{n-c}). \\tag{8.18}\n\\]\nLower-dimensional and embedded terms affect only higher components. Thus the required universal cycle classes are locally constant.</p><p>Form \\(H_T=H^{an}\\times_{S^{an}}T\\), proper over \\(T\\). The locus where the chosen \\(b\\) rational combinations lie in and span \\(\\mathcal V\\) is open and closed, because it is a condition on fixed rational vectors in local system trivializations. It is a closed analytic subspace, proper over \\(T\\). Reduce it and apply Remmert’s proper-image theorem; its image is closed analytic.</p><p>The countably many such images cover exactly the algebraicity locus. Any proper closed analytic subset of connected \\(T\\) is nowhere dense. The nonempty assumed open is Baire, so one image must have interior and therefore equal \\(T\\). This proves the assertion.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 14.5", "orig": "Proposition 8.4 (Every hyperbolic rational lattice)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For fixed \\(K=\\mathbb Q(u)\\), \\(u^2=-d\\), \\(d\\geq3\\), every complex abelian sixfold with unital \\(K\\)-action and compatible polarization whose actual homological Hermitian form is hyperbolic has algebraic rational Weil …", "statement": "<p>For fixed \\(K=\\mathbb Q(u)\\), \\(u^2=-d\\), \\(d\\geq3\\), every complex abelian sixfold with unital \\(K\\)-action and compatible polarization whose actual homological Hermitian form is hyperbolic has algebraic rational Weil plane.</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemma 14.2 and the algebraic polarization cube make\n\\[\nw_I=\\kappa_{B,3}(I)-c_\\theta\\theta_I^3\n\\]\nalgebraic near the seed. It is nonzero everywhere as a marked flat vector. Lemma 14.3 gives the whole plane there. The fiberwise algebraic isogenies from Lemma 14.1 pull it to \\(W_K(A_I^{\\mathrm{pp}})\\).</p><p>Apply Lemma 14.4 to the universal fine level-four family, \\(T=\\mathcal D\\), \\(c=3\\), and the constant rank-two subsystem defined by the marked \\(K\\)-eigenspaces. Thus \\(W_K(A_I^{\\mathrm{pp}})\\) is algebraic for every \\(I\\in\\mathcal D\\).</p><p>For an arbitrary \\(A\\), Proposition 4 gives a \\(K\\)-isometry\n\\[\nf_A:(H_1(A,\\mathbb Q),h_A)\\simeq(V_0,h_0).\n\\]\nThe Riemann form is exactly the coefficient of \\(u\\) in the Hermitian form, so this is also a Riemann-form isometry. Transporting the complex structure gives a point \\(I\\in\\mathcal D\\).</p><p>The lattices \\(f_A(H_1(A,\\mathbb Z))\\) and \\(\\Lambda_{\\mathrm{pp}}\\) are commensurable. Clear denominators to obtain a complex-linear map \\(n_Af_A\\) inducing a holomorphic isogeny \\(A\\to A_I^{\\mathrm{pp}}\\), algebraic by GAGA. It is \\(K\\)-linear up to isogeny, so its first-cohomology pullback identifies both embedding spaces and hence both sixth exterior lines. Algebraic pullback proves the result.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 15", "orig": "Lemma 3.3 (Quadratic determinant supply)", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Both embedding determinant lines are CM-supplied if either:", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(A\\) have an imaginary-quadratic \\(E\\)-action and a compatible rational Riemann form.</p><p class=\"li\">Let its Hermitian signature be \\((p,q)\\), \\(n=\\dim A=p+q\\).</p><p class=\"li\">For an embedding \\(\\sigma:E\\hookrightarrow k\\), use the determinant on separate slots \\(D(H^1(A,k)_\\sigma)\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Both embedding determinant lines are CM-supplied if either:</p><p class=\"li num\">1. \\(n=6\\), \\((p,q)=(3,3)\\), and the form is hyperbolic; or</p><p class=\"li num\">2. \\(n&lt;6\\) and \\(p,q\\leq3\\).</p><p>The second case permits every compatible rational form; both cases hold at every period.</p>", "proof": "<p>In the first case, Proposition 14 algebraizes the rational Weil plane, hence both scalar determinant lines in \\(H^6(A,k)\\). To separate slots, use addition \\(\\mu:A^6\\to A\\) and the algebraic degree-one projector\n\\[\n\\pi_1^A=\n\\prod_{\\substack{0\\leq j\\leq2\\dim A\\\\j\\neq1}}\n\\frac{[t]^*-t^j\\operatorname{id}}{t-t^j},\n\\quad t\\geq2.\n\\]\nFor a determinant basis,\n\\[\n(\\pi_1^A)^{\\boxtimes6}\\mu^*(v_1\\wedge\\cdots\\wedge v_6)\n=\n\\sum_{\\tau\\in S_6}\\operatorname{sgn}(\\tau)\nv_{\\tau(1)}\\otimes\\cdots\\otimes v_{\\tau(6)},\n\\]\na nonzero separate-slot determinant. It is algebraic over \\(k\\), hence supplied from a point.</p><p>In the second case, adjoin \\(3-p\\) CM elliptic \\(E\\)-lines of signature \\((1,0)\\) and \\(3-q\\) of signature \\((0,1)\\). Their Hermitian coefficients have arbitrary positive rational magnitudes with prescribed signs. The orthogonal product \\(X\\) has signature \\((3,3)\\) and negative determinant \\(d_0\\). At least one elliptic line was added; multiply its coefficient by \\(-1/d_0&gt;0\\), making determinant \\(-1\\). Proposition 4 makes the actual form hyperbolic. Clearing denominators preserves its determinant class, since rank-six rational scaling is a norm.</p><p>The first case supplies the full determinant of\n\\[\nH^1(A,k)_\\sigma\\oplus L_1\\oplus\\cdots\\oplus L_{6-n}.\n\\]\nAlgebraic block projection and alternation give its nonzero factor\n\\[\nD(H^1(A,k)_\\sigma)\\otimes L_1\\otimes\\cdots\\otimes L_{6-n}.\n\\]\nThe opposite elliptic lines are supplied by CM curves. Tensor them and contract the perfect elliptic pairings, using Proposition 1, to remove the \\(L_j\\). The resulting nonzero determinant is CM-supplied; scalar conjugation gives the other orientation.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 16", "orig": "Lemma 3.8 (Independence of the small columns), with the column classification of Lemmas 3.4–3.7", "depth": 0, "snippet": "The columns and pairings are those in Lemma 16.4 below.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(A\\) have an imaginary-quadratic \\(E\\)-action and \\(\\dim A\\leq6\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Write \\(A\\sim\\prod_BB^{r_B}\\), with pairwise nonisogenous simple \\(B\\), and discard CM factors.</p><p class=\"li\">For a remaining \\(B\\), let \\(g=\\dim B\\), \\(J_B=\\operatorname{Hdg}(H_B)\\), \\(R_B=J_B^{\\mathrm{der}}\\), \\(H_B=H^1(B,\\mathbb Q)\\), \\(D=\\operatorname{End}_{\\mathrm{Hdg}}(H_B)\\).</p><p class=\"li\">A column is the image of a primitive matrix idempotent after splitting \\(D_k\\). Matrix copies are admissibly identified; opposite Type IV embeddings are paired.</p><p class=\"li\">A column is mixed if it has both Hodge-circle weights \\(1,-1\\), and pure otherwise.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The columns and pairings are those in Lemma 16.4 below. Their classical derived projections are independent after identifying matrix copies and pairing opposite embeddings. Thus these data satisfy assumptions (a)–(c) of Proposition 2, with \\(H_0\\) the sum of CM isotypic factors.</p>", "proof": "<p>Split each \\(SO_4\\) target into two simple ideals. A semisimple subdirect Lie algebra of the simple targets can fail to be the product only through a graph link identifying isomorphic target ideals. This link relation is Galois-invariant.</p><p>First consider columns mixed in every scalar conjugate, excluding the special \\(SO_4\\) and \\(SO_6\\simeq SL_4\\) representation issues. Isomorphic simple targets identify their standard modules, up to replacing an \\(SL\\)-column by its opposite. A derived intertwiner line is stable under the full Hodge group. Its circle character shifts the spectrum \\(\\{-1,1\\}\\) to itself, so is trivial, also after every conjugation. Coefficient descent makes it a full Hodge endomorphism. The actual commutant can mix only matrix copies within one central embedding and simple isogeny type, contradicting distinct chosen blocks.</p><p>The unequal standard representation issue \\(SO_6\\simeq SL_4\\) cannot give a link: the Type III sixfold exhausts \\(A\\).</p><p>For partially pure Type IV factors, the \\(f=2\\) or \\(3\\) columns above one \\(E\\)-embedding form a prime-sized transitive Galois set. An invariant equivalence relation is discrete or universal. The traceless circle differential vanishes on pure columns and not on mixed columns; graph links preserve this distinction. The universal relation is impossible, so their own ideals are independent.</p><p>Only the quartic-center fourfold has room for an external non-CM partner. Its two \\(A_1\\)-ideals are Galois-interchanged. A non-CM \\(A_1\\)-target in the remaining dimension at most two is either a repeated non-CM elliptic factor or a Type II surface, with a single Galois-stable absolute target. Linking it to one of the pair links it to both, contradicting their independence.</p><p>For \\(SO_4\\), the column is \\(k^2\\otimes k^2\\). If both simple-factor circle differentials were nonzero, spectra \\(\\{\\pm a\\pm b\\}\\) would have at least three values. Exactly one is nonzero, since the column is mixed. The two ideals must be Galois-interchanged; otherwise the inactive one would descend to a rational adjoint quotient trivial on the circle, violating minimality. The same remainder argument excludes external links.</p><p>Thus the Lie image is the full product. A connected closed subgroup of the connected classical product with full Lie algebra is the whole product. Lemma 16.4 and Proposition 1 give the remaining data. No independence of central characters is asserted.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 16.1", "orig": "Lemma 3.4 (Albert columns and their pairings)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For simple non-CM \\(B\\), the following are necessary column data:", "statement": "<p>For simple non-CM \\(B\\), the following are necessary column data:\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c|c|c|c}\n\\text{type}&amp;\\text{divisibility}&amp;\\dim W&amp;\\text{pair}\\\\ \\hline\nI&amp;f\\mid g&amp;2g/f&amp;\\text{alternating self-pair}\\\\\nII&amp;2f\\mid g&amp;g/f&amp;\\text{alternating self-pair}\\\\\nIII&amp;2f\\mid g&amp;g/f&amp;\\text{symmetric self-pair}\\\\\nIV&amp;fb^2\\mid g&amp;g/(fb)&amp;\\text{opposite-column pair}.\n\\end{array}\n\\]\nIn Types I–III the center is totally real of degree \\(f\\); in Type IV it is CM of degree \\(2f\\), and \\([D:Z(D)]=b^2\\). All projectors, copy maps, and pair tensors are admissible. Opposite Type IV columns are derived duals.</p>", "proof": "<p>The degree-one realization identifies \\(D\\) with the opposite of \\(\\operatorname{End}^0(B)\\) and with the commutant of \\(J_B\\). Complete reducibility and the split commutant give\n\\[\nD_k=\\prod_{\\tau:Z(D)\\hookrightarrow k}M_s(k),\n\\quad\nH_{B,k}=\\bigoplus_\\tau W_\\tau\\otimes U_\\tau,\n\\quad\\dim U_\\tau=s.\n\\]\nPrimitive idempotents and matrix units are admissible. Columns are irreducible under \\(R_B\\), because the connected center acts by scalars.</p><p>Albert divisibilities follow from \\(H_B\\) being a \\(D\\)-module. On a first-kind central block, factor the polarization as\n\\[\n\\psi|_{W\\otimes U}=\\beta_W\\otimes b_U.\n\\]\nThe adjoint relation for all matrices forces this factorization: writing another multiplicity form as \\(b_U(S-, -)\\), \\(S\\) commutes with every matrix and is scalar.</p><p>In Type I, \\(b_U\\) is symmetric. Positive first-kind quaternionic involution is orthogonal in Type II and canonical symplectic in Type III. The alternative signs violate positivity: a symplectic adjoint on \\(M_2(\\mathbb R)\\) gives \\(\\operatorname{Tr}(ee^\\dagger)=0\\) for a rank-one idempotent; a noncanonical involution on Hamilton quaternions fixes a pure quaternion with negative reduced square trace. Since \\(\\psi\\) is alternating, \\(\\beta_W\\) is alternating in I, II and symmetric in III.</p><p>For I, II restrict to a multiplicity line nonisotropic for \\(b_U\\), then transport to the chosen column. For III use two multiplicity lines with nonzero alternating pairing and identify the column copies by a matrix unit. The inverse tensors pair complementary Hodge degrees in every scalar conjugate. Type I’s alternating dimension gives \\(f\\mid g\\).</p><p>In Type IV, Rosati is complex conjugation on the CM center, so the polarization pairs opposite embeddings. Factor the split matrix pairing and use Proposition 1’s \\(W\\)-to-\\(\\overline W\\) pair, transported to the chosen opposite column. This gives the derived duality.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 16.2", "orig": "Lemma 3.5 (Two circle weights)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Every nontrivial simple-factor representation in the irreducible tensor decomposition of a column for \\(\\operatorname{Lie}(R_B)_k\\) is minuscule.", "statement": "<p>Every nontrivial simple-factor representation in the irreducible tensor decomposition of a column for \\(\\operatorname{Lie}(R_B)_k\\) is minuscule.</p><p>The simple nontrivial minuscule modules of dimensions at most six needed here are:\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c|l}\n2&amp;A_1\\text{ standard, alternating}\\\\\n3&amp;A_2\\text{ standard and dual}\\\\\n4&amp;A_3\\text{ standard and dual};\\ C_2=B_2\\text{ standard/spin, alternating}\\\\\n5&amp;A_4\\text{ standard and dual}\\\\\n6&amp;A_5\\text{ standard and dual};\\ C_3\\text{ standard, alternating};\n\\ A_3\\text{ on }\\bigwedge^2k^4,\\text{ symmetric}.\n\\end{array}\n\\]\nUnmarked standard modules are nonselfdual.</p>", "proof": "<p>Each Galois orbit of simple ideals has some nonzero circle-differential projection. Otherwise its rational adjoint quotient would be trivial on the circle, contradicting minimality of \\(J_B\\).</p><p>For an acting ideal, simultaneously scalar-conjugate it and its column so this projection \\(x\\) is nonzero. Fix eigenvectors in all other tensor factors. Since the full column has at most the two circle weights, \\(x\\) has at most two eigenvalues on its simple-factor module. It has at least two: the representation is faithful, and a scalar traceless semisimple operator is zero.</p><p>Choose a Cartan containing \\(x\\). For every root \\(\\alpha\\), a Weyl conjugate \\(x'\\) has \\(\\alpha(x')\\neq0\\), because the Weyl orbit of a root spans the dual Cartan. If a highest weight \\(\\lambda\\) had \\(\\langle\\lambda,\\alpha^\\vee\\rangle\\geq2\\), its root string would give three distinct eigenvalues on \\(x'\\), impossible. Thus the representation is minuscule; conjugation transports this back. The displayed table is the small minuscule classification used in the paper.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 16.3", "orig": "Lemma 3.6 (Small derived projections)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "An alternating self-paired column of dimension \\(2,4,6\\) has full symplectic derived image.", "statement": "<p>An alternating self-paired column of dimension \\(2,4,6\\) has full symplectic derived image. A symmetric self-paired column cannot have dimension two; in dimensions four and six its image is \\(SO_4,SO_6\\).</p><p>If \\(D=K\\) is imaginary quadratic, \\(B\\) is non-CM, and \\(\\dim_KH_B\\leq6\\), each embedding column has full special linear derived image, with the dual on the opposite column.</p>", "proof": "<p>A self-paired column is derived-selfdual, so every simple tensor factor is selfdual. The only possible nontrivial tensor dimensions at most six are \\(2\\cdot2\\), \\(2\\cdot3\\). The latter is not selfdual; the former is a tensor product of two alternating \\(A_1\\)-standards and is symmetric. Lemma 16.2 therefore gives exactly the asserted symplectic images and the symmetric \\(2\\otimes2\\) and \\(\\bigwedge^2k^4\\) images, namely \\(SO_4,SO_6\\). A connected subgroup of \\(SO_2\\) is toric and cannot act irreducibly over \\(k\\) in dimension two. Connectedness turns the Lie images into the stated groups.</p><p>For quadratic \\(K\\), both columns \\(W,\\overline W\\) are mixed; otherwise the circle lies in the rational \\(K\\)-scalar torus and \\(B\\) is CM. If \\(W\\) were derived-selfdual, \\(\\operatorname{Hom}_{R_B}(W,\\overline W)\\) would be a one-dimensional full-group-stable line of isomorphisms. Its circle character shifts one entire spectrum to the other. Both spectra are \\(\\{-1,1\\}\\), so the shift is zero, also in every conjugate. Coefficient descent then gives an endomorphism joining distinct central embeddings, contradicting the commutant \\(K_k\\).</p><p>Every simple ideal acts on \\(W\\), since \\(W\\oplus\\overline W\\) is faithful and opposite actions are dual. At most one ideal has nonzero circle differential: two nonconstant factor spectra would have a sumset of at least three values. Every Galois orbit has an active ideal, so all ideals form one orbit and have the same type. The only nonselfdual tensor candidate \\(2\\otimes3\\) has types \\(A_1,A_2\\) and is excluded. The table leaves only standard or dual special linear modules.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 16.4", "orig": "Lemma 3.7 (The small column list)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For the \\(A\\) of Proposition 16, after discarding CM factors and identifying matrix copies:", "statement": "<p>For the \\(A\\) of Proposition 16, after discarding CM factors and identifying matrix copies:</p><p class=\"li num\">1. Types I and II have full symplectic columns of dimensions \\(2,4,6\\).</p><p class=\"li num\">2. Type III has \\(g=4\\) or \\(6\\), center \\(\\mathbb Q\\), \\(r_B=1\\), and a unique column with full \\(SO_g\\) image. Each \\(E\\)-eigenspace is one column copy, of Hodge multiplicities \\((g/2,g/2)\\).</p><p class=\"li num\">3. Type IV has \\(D=K\\) a CM field. If \\(E\\) does not embed in \\(K\\), then \\(K\\) is imaginary quadratic, \\(g=3,r_B=2\\), and the \\(K\\)-columns have \\(SL_3\\) and dual images.</p><p class=\"li num\">4. If \\(E\\) embeds in \\(K\\), write \\([K:\\mathbb Q]=2f\\), \\(a=g/f\\). The possibilities are\n   \\[\n   f=1,\\quad3\\leq a=g\\leq6;\n   \\qquad(f,a,g)=(2,2,4),(2,3,6),(3,2,6).\n   \\]\n   Every column has full \\(SL_a\\) image. When \\(a=2\\), some embedding pairs are mixed and some pure.</p><p>These are projections of the actual derived Hodge group of \\(A\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>The field action restricts injectively to each isotypic matrix algebra.</p><p>In Type I, \\(F\\otimes_{\\mathbb Q}E\\) is a quadratic field over totally real \\(F\\), acting on \\(F^{r_B}\\). Thus \\(r_B\\) is even and \\(g\\leq3\\); Lemmas 16.1, 16.3 give dimensions \\(2,4,6\\). Type II follows from \\(2f\\mid g\\leq6\\). In Type III, the symmetric dimension two is excluded; the remaining dimensions and \\(gr_B\\leq6\\) force \\(f=r_B=1\\). The split injection \\(E_k=k\\times k\\hookrightarrow M_2(k)\\) has two rank-one idempotents, so each eigenspace is one copy of the self-paired column, hence balanced.</p><p>For Type IV, \\(b&gt;1\\) and \\(fb^2\\mid g\\leq6\\) leave only \\((g,f,b,\\dim W)=(4,1,2,2)\\). A two-dimensional column cannot have every conjugate mixed: its determinant would then be an all-conjugate \\((1,1)\\) alternating pair. Composing its inverse with the opposite polarization pair gives an admissible map \\(W\\to\\overline W\\), contradicting distinct center embeddings in the commutant. If all columns are pure, the circle is in the center-scalar torus, making \\(B\\) CM. Thus non-CM rank-two columns require both pure and mixed pairs; with \\(f=1\\) this excludes the noncommutative tuple and the field case \\(a=2\\). Rank-one columns likewise give CM.</p><p>Hence \\(D=K\\). If \\(E\\) does not embed, \\(K\\otimes E\\) is a quadratic field over \\(K\\), forcing \\(r_B\\) even and \\(g\\leq3\\). The exclusions leave the stated quadratic threefold repeated twice. If \\(E\\) embeds, \\(K\\otimes E=K\\times K\\); diagonalizing its two idempotents only reassigns matrix copies to the \\(E\\)-eigenspaces, allowing a noncentral action. The bounds give exactly the listed tuples. Quadratic-center cases use Lemma 16.3; higher-center columns have dimensions two or three, so Lemma 16.2 forces \\(SL_2\\) or \\(SL_3\\), including pure columns by conjugation.</p><p>Finally, restriction of the actual Hodge group of \\(A\\) to \\(H_B\\) has image exactly \\(J_B\\): its image contains the circle, and the inverse image of \\(J_B\\) contains the circle of \\(A\\); minimality gives equality. Derived projection is therefore surjective.</p>"}], "edges": [[2, 0, "cite"], [2, 1, "cite"], [2, 7, "cite"], [2, 9, "cite"], [2, 49, "cite"], [2, 50, "cite"], [2, 51, "cite"], [3, 2, "cite"], [4, 2, "cite"], [4, 6, "cite"], [5, 2, "cite"], [6, 2, "cite"], [7, 0, "cite"], [7, 1, "cite"], [7, 9, "cite"], [7, 50, "cite"], [8, 7, "cite"], [10, 48, "cite"], [10, 49, "cite"], [11, 15, "cite"], [12, 13, "cite"], [13, 11, "cite"], [14, 11, "cite"], [14, 17, "cite"], [14, 18, "cite"], [15, 24, "cite"], [15, 30, "cite"], [15, 36, "cite"], [16, 15, "cite"], [16, 17, "cite"], [16, 30, "cite"], [17, 15, "cite"], [18, 15, "cite"], [19, 22, "cite"], [19, 24, "cite"], [20, 19, "cite"], [21, 19, "cite"], [22, 24, "cite"], [23, 22, "cite"], [24, 36, "cite"], [24, 37, "cite"], [25, 26, "cite"], [26, 27, "cite"], [27, 24, "cite"], [28, 24, "part"], [29, 24, "cite"], [30, 44, "cite"], [31, 30, "cite"], [31, 32, "cite"], [32, 30, "cite"], [32, 35, "cite"], [33, 30, "cite"], [34, 30, "cite"], [34, 35, "cite"], [35, 30, "cite"], [36, 38, "cite"], [36, 44, "cite"], [37, 36, "cite"], [38, 45, "cite"], [39, 45, "cite"], [40, 41, "cite"], [40, 42, "cite"], [41, 39, "cite"], [42, 39, "cite"], [43, 49, "cite"], [44, 45, "cite"], [44, 48, "cite"], [45, 48, "cite"], [46, 48, "cite"], [47, 48, "cite"], [48, 43, "cite"], [49, 0, "cite"], [49, 1, "cite"], [50, 0, "cite"], [50, 1, "cite"], [51, 54, "cite"], [52, 53, "cite"], [52, 54, "cite"], [53, 54, "cite"], [54, 0, "cite"], [54, 50, "cite"]]});
